LM.PAGES = {"pages":{"a1/functions-asking-to-repeat.md":"---\nid: a1-functions-asking-to-repeat\nlevel: A1\ncategory: functions\ntitle: Survival phrases: ask people to repeat, slow down, explain\nsummary: The phrases that keep a conversation going when you don't understand: repeat, slower, how do you say, what does it mean.\n---\n\n# Survival phrases: ask people to repeat, slow down, explain\n\nReal Spanish is fast, and you will not understand everything. That is normal, and these phrases are your safety net. They let you keep talking instead of going silent. Most of them use *poder* (can): *¿Puede repetir?* (formal) or *¿Puedes repetir?* (informal). Learn both versions, and keep the formal one for strangers, shops and offices.\n\n## Key phrases\n\n**When you don't understand**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Perdón, no entiendo. | Sorry, I don't understand. |\n| ¿Puede repetir, por favor? / ¿Puedes repetir? | Can you repeat that, please? |\n| Más despacio, por favor. (also *más lento*, common in Latin America) | Slower, please. |\n| ¿Puede hablar más despacio? / ¿Puedes hablar más despacio? | Can you speak more slowly? |\n| ¿Cómo? / ¿Perdón? | Sorry? (I didn't hear) |\n| No hablo mucho español. | I don't speak much Spanish. |\n| ¿Habla inglés? / ¿Hablas inglés? | Do you speak English? |\n\n**Asking about words**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Cómo se dice \"key\" en español? | How do you say \"key\" in Spanish? |\n| ¿Qué significa \"llave\"? | What does \"llave\" mean? |\n| ¿Cómo se escribe? | How do you spell it? |\n| ¿Cómo se pronuncia? | How do you pronounce it? |\n| ¿Puede escribirlo, por favor? | Can you write it down, please? |\n\n**Checking and thanking**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Así? | Like this? |\n| ¿Es correcto? | Is that right? |\n| Ah, vale. (Spain) / Ah, okey. / Ah, ya entiendo. | Oh, OK. / Oh, I get it now. |\n| Muchas gracias por su paciencia. | Thank you very much for your patience. |\n\n## Dialogue\n\nAt a train station ticket office.\n\n> **Clerk:** Buenos días. ¿Adónde va? (Good morning. Where are you going?)\n> **Tom:** Perdón, no entiendo. ¿Puede repetir, por favor? (Sorry, I don't understand. Can you repeat that, please?)\n> **Clerk:** Claro. ¿A qué ciudad va? (Of course. Which city are you going to?)\n> **Tom:** A Toledo. ¿Cómo se dice \"return ticket\" en español? (To Toledo. How do you say \"return ticket\" in Spanish?)\n> **Clerk:** Ida y vuelta. (Round trip.)\n> **Tom:** Ah, vale. Un billete de ida y vuelta, por favor. (Oh, OK. A round-trip ticket, please.)\n> **Clerk:** Son veinte euros con cuarenta. (That's twenty euros forty.)\n> **Tom:** Más despacio, por favor. (Slower, please.)\n> **Clerk:** Veinte… euros… con cuarenta. (Twenty… euros… forty.)\n> **Tom:** Muchas gracias. (Thank you very much.)\n\n## Culture note\n\n- *Vale* is the Spain word for \"OK\"; in much of Latin America people say *está bien*, *bueno*, *ok* or *dale* (Argentina, Uruguay).\n- A ticket for a train or a bus is *un billete* in Spain and *un boleto* in most of Latin America.\n- People are usually happy to repeat. Showing you are trying is more important than speaking perfectly.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *No comprendo nada, repite.* (to a stranger) → ✓ *Perdón, no entiendo. ¿Puede repetir?* — too direct with a stranger: use *usted* and a question.\n- ✗ *¿Qué significa en español \"key\"?* → ✓ *¿Cómo se dice \"key\" en español?* — *significa* asks for the meaning of a word you heard.\n- ✗ *¿Puede repetir, por favor.* → ✓ *¿Puede repetir, por favor?* — a question opens with ¿ and closes with ?.\n\n## Practice\n\nWhat do you say?\n\n1. You don't understand.\n2. Someone speaks too fast. (to a stranger)\n3. You want to know how to say \"bread\" in Spanish.\n4. You want to know what \"llave\" means.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. Can you repeat that, please? (formal)\n6. How do you spell it?\n7. Can you write it down, please? (formal)\n8. I don't speak much Spanish.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. Perdón, no entiendo. / No entiendo.\n2. ¿Puede hablar más despacio, por favor? / Más despacio, por favor.\n3. ¿Cómo se dice \"bread\" en español?\n4. ¿Qué significa \"llave\"?\n5. ¿Puede repetir, por favor?\n6. ¿Cómo se escribe?\n7. ¿Puede escribirlo, por favor?\n8. No hablo mucho español.\n","a1/functions-greetings-introductions.md":"---\nid: a1-functions-greetings-introductions\nlevel: A1\ncategory: functions\ntitle: Greet people, say goodbye and introduce yourself\nsummary: Say hello at any time of day, give your name, ask someone's name, react politely and say goodbye.\n---\n\n# Greet people, say goodbye and introduce yourself\n\nThese are the first sentences you will use, and you can learn them as fixed phrases before you know the grammar inside them. One difference from English matters from day one: Spanish has two ways to say \"you\". Use *tú* with friends, family, children and people your age; use *usted* with older people, strangers in formal situations, and customers or senior people at work. Many phrases below come in both versions.\n\n## Key phrases\n\n**Saying hello**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Hola. | Hi. / Hello. (any time, any person) |\n| Buenos días. | Good morning. (until noon in Latin America; until lunch, about 2 p.m., in Spain) |\n| Buenas tardes. | Good afternoon. / Good evening. (until it gets dark) |\n| Buenas noches. | Good evening. / Good night. (after dark, also to say goodbye) |\n| ¿Qué tal? | How's it going? (very common, friendly) |\n| ¿Cómo estás? / ¿Cómo está usted? | How are you? (informal / formal) |\n| Bien, gracias. ¿Y tú? / ¿Y usted? | Fine, thanks. And you? |\n\n**Introducing yourself and others**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Me llamo Ana. | My name is Ana. |\n| Soy Ana. | I'm Ana. |\n| ¿Cómo te llamas? | What's your name? (informal) |\n| ¿Cómo se llama usted? | What's your name? (formal) |\n| Este es Pedro. / Esta es Lucía. | This is Pedro. / This is Lucía. |\n| Mucho gusto. | Nice to meet you. |\n| Encantado. / Encantada. | Pleased to meet you. (a man says *encantado*, a woman *encantada*) |\n| Igualmente. | Likewise. |\n\n**Saying goodbye**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Adiós. | Goodbye. |\n| Hasta luego. | See you later. (also for \"bye\" in a shop) |\n| Hasta mañana. | See you tomorrow. |\n| Nos vemos. | See you. (informal) |\n| Chao. | Bye. (informal, common in Latin America) |\n\n## Dialogue\n\nAt a language exchange, two people meet.\n\n> **Marta:** ¡Hola! Me llamo Marta. ¿Y tú? (Hi! My name is Marta. And you?)\n> **Tom:** Hola, Marta. Soy Tom. (Hi, Marta. I'm Tom.)\n> **Marta:** Mucho gusto, Tom. (Nice to meet you, Tom.)\n> **Tom:** Igualmente. ¿Qué tal? (Likewise. How's it going?)\n> **Marta:** Muy bien, gracias. Mira, esta es Elena. (Very well, thanks. Look, this is Elena.)\n> **Elena:** Hola, Tom. Encantada. (Hi, Tom. Pleased to meet you.)\n> **Tom:** Encantado, Elena. (Pleased to meet you, Elena.)\n> **Marta:** Bueno, hasta luego. (OK, see you later.)\n> **Tom and Elena:** ¡Hasta luego! (See you later!)\n\n## Culture note\n\n- In Spain friends and new acquaintances often greet with two kisses, one on each cheek; in much of Latin America one kiss is usual. Men usually shake hands with each other.\n- *Buenas* on its own is a friendly short form of *buenos días* or *buenas tardes*.\n- When in doubt, start with *usted*. People will tell you if they prefer *tú*: *Puedes tutearme* (\"you can say *tú* to me\").\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Buenos tardes.* → ✓ *Buenas tardes.* — *tarde* and *noche* are feminine words, so it is *buenas*; only *días* takes *buenos*.\n- ✗ *Me llamo es Ana.* → ✓ *Me llamo Ana.* — *me llamo* already means \"my name is\"; there is no extra verb.\n- ✗ *Encantado* (said by a woman) → ✓ *Encantada* — the word agrees with the person speaking.\n- ✗ *¿Cómo te llamas?* (to an older stranger at an office) → ✓ *¿Cómo se llama usted?* — use the formal version with people you would call \"sir\" or \"madam\".\n- ✗ *Bien, y tú?* → ✓ *Bien, ¿y tú?* — a question inside a sentence still opens with ¿.\n\n## Practice\n\nChoose the right greeting.\n\n1. It is 9 a.m. You walk into a bakery: ___ (Buenos días / Buenas noches)\n2. It is 4 p.m.: ___ (Buenas tardes / Buenos tardes)\n\nComplete the dialogue.\n\n3. —Hola, me ___ Carlos. —Mucho gusto. Yo soy Rosa.\n4. —Encantada. —___. (Likewise.)\n5. —¿Qué tal? —Bien, gracias. ¿Y ___? (to a friend)\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n6. What's your name? (to your teacher's grandmother)\n7. See you tomorrow!\n8. Pleased to meet you. (said by a woman)\n\n## Answers\n\n1. Buenos días\n2. Buenas tardes\n3. llamo\n4. Igualmente\n5. tú\n6. ¿Cómo se llama usted? / ¿Cómo se llama?\n7. ¡Hasta mañana!\n8. Encantada. / Mucho gusto.\n","a1/functions-ordering-shopping.md":"---\nid: a1-functions-ordering-shopping\nlevel: A1\ncategory: functions\ntitle: Order in a café and buy things\nsummary: Order food and drinks, ask for the bill, and buy things in a shop politely.\n---\n\n# Order in a café and buy things\n\nIn a café or a shop a few polite phrases do everything. Spanish speakers often order with a simple verb: *Un café, por favor* or *Ponme un café* (Spain, informal). The most polite way for a learner is *Quería…* (I'd like…), a past form used here as a soft request; you do not need to know the tense to use it. Waiters often use *usted*, and customers answer with *usted* too.\n\n## Key phrases\n\n**Ordering**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Quería un café con leche, por favor. | I'd like a coffee with milk, please. |\n| Para mí, un té. / Yo, un agua. | For me, a tea. / I'll have a water. |\n| ¿Me pone un bocadillo de queso? (Spain) | Can I have a cheese sandwich? |\n| ¿Me da una empanada? | Can I have an empanada? |\n| ¿Qué tiene de comer? | What do you have to eat? |\n| ¿Tiene algo sin carne? | Do you have anything without meat? |\n| Una botella de agua sin gas / con gas. | A bottle of still / sparkling water. |\n\n**The waiter asks**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Qué va a tomar? | What will you have? |\n| ¿Algo más? | Anything else? |\n| ¿Para tomar aquí o para llevar? | For here or to go? |\n\n**Paying**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| La cuenta, por favor. | The check, please. |\n| ¿Cuánto es? | How much is it? |\n| ¿Me cobra, por favor? | Can I pay, please? |\n| ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? | Can I pay by card? |\n| En efectivo. | In cash. |\n\n**In a shop** (learn these as fixed phrases)\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Estoy buscando una camiseta. | I'm looking for a T-shirt. (*playera* in Mexico, *remera* in Argentina) |\n| ¿Cuánto cuesta esta? | How much is this one? |\n| ¿La tiene en azul? | Do you have it in blue? |\n| Me la llevo. | I'll take it. |\n| Solo estoy mirando, gracias. | I'm just looking, thanks. |\n\n## Dialogue\n\nIn a café in the morning.\n\n> **Waiter:** Buenos días. ¿Qué va a tomar? (Good morning. What will you have?)\n> **Laura:** Quería un café con leche y una tostada, por favor. (I'd like a coffee with milk and a piece of toast, please.)\n> **Waiter:** ¿Con tomate o con mantequilla? (With tomato or with butter?)\n> **Laura:** Con tomate. (With tomato.)\n> **Waiter:** Muy bien. ¿Algo más? (Very well. Anything else?)\n> **Laura:** No, nada más, gracias. (No, nothing else, thanks.)\n> **Waiter:** Muy bien. (Very well.)\n> **Laura:** La cuenta, por favor. ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? (The check, please. Can I pay by card?)\n> **Waiter:** Sí, claro. Son tres euros con cincuenta. (Yes, of course. It's three euros fifty.)\n\n## Culture note\n\n- A *bocadillo* in Spain is a sandwich in a baguette; a *sándwich* is made with sliced bread. In Mexico a similar sandwich is a *torta*.\n- In Spain you often pay at the end at the bar, and tips are small; in Mexico about 10–15% is usual.\n- *Por favor* and *gracias* are used less often than in English in quick exchanges (especially in Spain); a friendly tone does the same job.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Quiero tener un café.* → ✓ *Quería un café.* / *Un café, por favor.* — you just name what you want.\n- ✗ *Para yo, un café.* → ✓ *Para mí, un café.* — after *para*, *yo* becomes *mí*.\n- ✗ *¿Puedo pagar por tarjeta?* → ✓ *¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?* — you pay *con* a card, *en* cash.\n- ✗ *Una agua, por favor.* → ✓ *Un agua, por favor.* — *agua* is feminine but takes *un* / *el*.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite what you say.\n\n1. You want a tea. (polite)\n2. You want the check.\n3. You ask if you can pay by card.\n4. The waiter says *¿Algo más?* You don't want anything else.\n\nComplete the dialogue.\n\n5. —¿Qué va a ___? —Un café, por favor.\n6. —¿Para tomar aquí o para ___? —Para tomar aquí.\n7. —¿Cuánto ___? —Son dos euros.\n8. Translate: I'm just looking, thanks.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. Quería un té, por favor. / Un té, por favor.\n2. La cuenta, por favor.\n3. ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?\n4. No, nada más, gracias.\n5. tomar\n6. llevar\n7. es\n8. Solo estoy mirando, gracias.\n","a1/functions-personal-details.md":"---\nid: a1-functions-personal-details\nlevel: A1\ncategory: functions\ntitle: Give and ask for personal details\nsummary: Ask and answer where you are from, how old you are, what you do, your phone number and e-mail, in both tú and usted.\n---\n\n# Give and ask for personal details\n\nForms, language courses, hotels and new friends all ask the same few questions. With *ser* and *tener* you can already answer most of them. Two things are different from English: \"What is your name / number?\" is *¿Cuál es…?*, not *¿Qué es…?*, and \"How old are you?\" uses *tener*: *¿Cuántos años tienes?* Learn each question in two versions: with *tú* for people your age and with *usted* at an office or a reception desk.\n\n## Key phrases\n\n**Asking (tú / usted)**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Cómo te llamas? / ¿Cómo se llama? | What's your name? |\n| ¿De dónde eres? / ¿De dónde es? | Where are you from? |\n| ¿Cuántos años tienes? / ¿Cuántos años tiene? | How old are you? |\n| ¿A qué te dedicas? / ¿A qué se dedica? | What do you do (for a living)? |\n| ¿Cuál es tu número de teléfono? / ¿Cuál es su número? | What's your phone number? |\n| ¿Cuál es tu correo electrónico? / ¿Cuál es su correo? | What's your e-mail address? |\n| ¿Cómo se escribe? | How do you spell it? |\n\n**Answering**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Me llamo Ana García. | My name is Ana García. |\n| Soy de Canadá. Soy canadiense. | I'm from Canada. I'm Canadian. |\n| Tengo veintiocho años. | I'm twenty-eight. |\n| Soy enfermera. / Soy estudiante. | I'm a nurse. / I'm a student. |\n| Mi número es el 6 47 31 25 08. | My number is 6 47 31 25 08. |\n| Es ana punto garcía arroba correo punto com. | It's ana.garcia@correo.com. |\n| Con tilde / sin tilde. | With / without an accent. |\n\n**Checking**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Perdón? ¿Puedes repetir? / ¿Puede repetir? | Sorry? Can you repeat that? |\n| ¿Y tú? / ¿Y usted? | And you? |\n\n## Dialogue\n\nAt the reception desk of a language school.\n\n> **Receptionist:** Buenos días. ¿Cómo se llama? (Good morning. What's your name?)\n> **Daniel:** Daniel Wright. (Daniel Wright.)\n> **Receptionist:** ¿Cómo se escribe Wright? (How do you spell Wright?)\n> **Daniel:** Uve doble, erre, i, ge, hache, te. (W, R, I, G, H, T.)\n> **Receptionist:** Gracias. ¿De dónde es? (Thank you. Where are you from?)\n> **Daniel:** Soy de Toronto. Soy canadiense. (I'm from Toronto. I'm Canadian.)\n> **Receptionist:** ¿Y cuántos años tiene? (And how old are you?)\n> **Daniel:** Treinta y cuatro. (Thirty-four.)\n> **Receptionist:** ¿Cuál es su número de teléfono? (What's your phone number?)\n> **Daniel:** Es el seis, cuarenta y siete, treinta y uno, veinticinco, cero ocho. (It's 6 47 31 25 08.)\n\n## Culture note\n\n- In Spain phone numbers are often read in pairs or threes; in many Latin American countries digit by digit is more common. Both are understood.\n- Spanish speakers have two family names, the father's and the mother's: *Ana García López*. On forms, *apellidos* means \"surnames\".\n- *@* is *arroba*, *.* is *punto*, *-* is *guion* and *_* is *guion bajo*. W is *uve doble* (Spain), *doble ve* or *doble u* (Latin America).\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *¿Qué es tu número?* → ✓ *¿Cuál es tu número?* — \"what is\" + a piece of information is *¿cuál es…?*\n- ✗ *¿Cuántos años eres?* → ✓ *¿Cuántos años tienes?* — age takes *tener*.\n- ✗ *Soy Argentino.* → ✓ *Soy argentino.* — nationalities take a small letter.\n- ✗ *Soy un estudiante.* → ✓ *Soy estudiante.* — no article before a job or role on its own.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the question for each answer (use *tú*).\n\n1. —Me llamo Sofía.\n2. —Soy de Chile.\n3. —Tengo diecinueve años.\n4. —Soy camarero.\n\nWrite the formal (*usted*) version.\n\n5. ¿De dónde eres?\n6. ¿Cuántos años tienes?\n7. ¿Cuál es tu número de teléfono?\n8. Translate: I'm a student and I'm twenty years old.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. ¿Cómo te llamas?\n2. ¿De dónde eres?\n3. ¿Cuántos años tienes?\n4. ¿A qué te dedicas?\n5. ¿De dónde es usted? / ¿De dónde es?\n6. ¿Cuántos años tiene usted? / ¿Cuántos años tiene?\n7. ¿Cuál es su número de teléfono?\n8. Soy estudiante y tengo veinte años.\n","a1/functions-time-dates.md":"---\nid: a1-functions-time-dates\nlevel: A1\ncategory: functions\ntitle: Tell the time, give dates and talk about schedules\nsummary: Ask and say the time, give the date, and ask when shops open and things start.\n---\n\n# Tell the time, give dates and talk about schedules\n\nTo tell the time Spanish uses *ser* and the article *la / las*: *Es la una. Son las tres.* \"At\" a time is *a la / a las*: *a las ocho*. Dates are simple: *el* + number + *de* + month. Two things differ from English: there is no word for \"o'clock\" (*en punto* only stresses \"exactly\"), and in Spain times after the half hour are usually said as \"to\" the next hour (*las cinco menos cuarto*). In daily life in Latin America, many people say the minutes after the hour instead (*las cuatro y cuarenta y cinco*) or *un cuarto para las cinco* (a quarter to five).\n\n## Key phrases\n\n**Asking and telling the time**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Qué hora es? | What time is it? |\n| Es la una. | It's one o'clock. |\n| Son las dos. / Son las diez. | It's two. / It's ten. |\n| Son las tres y cuarto. | It's a quarter past three. |\n| Son las tres y media. | It's half past three. |\n| Son las cuatro menos cuarto. | It's a quarter to four. |\n| Son las cinco y diez. / Son las seis menos veinte. | It's ten past five. / It's twenty to six. |\n| Es mediodía. / Es medianoche. | It's noon. / It's midnight. |\n| de la mañana / de la tarde / de la noche | a.m. / p.m. (afternoon) / p.m. (night) |\n\n**When? Schedules**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿A qué hora abre la farmacia? | What time does the pharmacy open? |\n| Abre a las nueve y cierra a las ocho. | It opens at nine and closes at eight. |\n| ¿A qué hora empieza la película? | What time does the movie start? |\n| A las diez en punto. | At ten o'clock sharp. |\n| de nueve a cinco | from nine to five |\n| por la mañana / por la tarde / por la noche | in the morning / in the afternoon / at night (Latin America also: *en la mañana*, *en la noche*) |\n\n**Dates**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Qué día es hoy? | What day is it today? |\n| Hoy es martes, 5 de mayo. | Today is Tuesday, May 5. |\n| ¿Cuándo es tu cumpleaños? | When is your birthday? |\n| Es el 12 de octubre. | It's October 12. |\n| el uno de enero / el primero de enero | January 1 |\n\n## Dialogue\n\nTwo friends plan to see a movie.\n\n> **Lucía:** ¿Qué hora es? (What time is it?)\n> **Javier:** Son las seis y media. (It's half past six.)\n> **Lucía:** ¿A qué hora empieza la película? (What time does the movie start?)\n> **Javier:** A las ocho menos cuarto. (At a quarter to eight.)\n> **Lucía:** Perfecto. ¿Y qué día es el concierto de Ana? (Perfect. And what day is Ana's concert?)\n> **Javier:** El sábado, 14 de junio, a las nueve de la noche. (On Saturday, June 14, at nine p.m.)\n> **Lucía:** ¡Genial! (Great!)\n\n## Culture note\n\n- Timetables, trains and shops use the 24-hour clock: *El tren sale a las 18:30* (*a las dieciocho treinta*).\n- In Spain lunch is often at 2 p.m. and dinner at 9 or 10 p.m.; *la tarde* lasts until it gets dark.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Son la una.* → ✓ *Es la una.* — one o'clock is singular: *es la una*.\n- ✗ *Es las tres.* → ✓ *Son las tres.* — from two o'clock on, the verb is *son*.\n- ✗ *en las ocho* → ✓ *a las ocho* — \"at\" a time is *a*.\n- ✗ *mayo 5* → ✓ *el 5 de mayo* — the day comes first, then *de* and the month.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the time in words.\n\n1. 1:00\n2. 3:15\n3. 7:30\n4. 9:45\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. What time is it?\n6. The bank opens at nine.\n7. My birthday is (on) March 3.\n8. at night\n\n## Answers\n\n1. Es la una.\n2. Son las tres y cuarto.\n3. Son las siete y media.\n4. Son las diez menos cuarto. / Son las nueve y cuarenta y cinco.\n5. ¿Qué hora es?\n6. El banco abre a las nueve.\n7. Mi cumpleaños es el 3 de marzo.\n8. por la noche / en la noche / de noche\n","a1/grammar-adjective-agreement.md":"---\nid: a1-grammar-adjective-agreement\nlevel: A1\ncategory: grammar\ntitle: Adjective agreement and position\nsummary: Spanish adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number, and they usually come after it: una casa blanca.\n---\n\n# Adjective agreement and position\n\nAn English adjective never changes: a white house, two white cars. A Spanish adjective follows its noun in two ways. It **agrees**: it takes the same gender and number as the noun. And it usually comes **after** the noun: *una casa blanca* is word for word \"a house white\".\n\n## How it works\n\n**Agreement.** Adjectives have up to four forms.\n\n| Adjective ends in | masculine | feminine | masculine plural | feminine plural |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **-o** | alt**o** | alt**a** | alt**os** | alt**as** |\n| **-e** | grand**e** | grand**e** | grand**es** | grand**es** |\n| a consonant | azul | azul | azul**es** | azul**es** |\n| nationality in a consonant | español | español**a** | español**es** | español**as** |\n\n- *-e* and most consonant adjectives have one form for both genders: *un coche grande*, *una casa grande*; *un libro fácil*, *una pregunta fácil*.\n- Nationalities in a consonant add *-a* for the feminine: *inglés* → *inglesa*, *alemán* → *alemana*. The accent disappears when a syllable is added.\n- A **mixed group** takes the masculine plural: *Juan y Ana son altos.*\n\n**Position.** Most adjectives go **after** the noun: *un coche rojo*, *una ciudad bonita*, *unos chicos simpáticos*.\n\nThese go **before** the noun:\n\n- numbers and quantities: *dos libros*, *mucho tiempo*, *pocas personas*;\n- *bueno* and *malo* often do, and they lose the *-o* before a masculine singular noun: *un buen amigo*, *un mal día*;\n- *grande* before a singular noun becomes *gran* and means \"great, important\": *un gran hombre* (a great man), *un hombre grande* (a big man).\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Describing things.** *Es un coche rojo.* (It's a red car.)\n2. **Describing people** with *ser*. *Mi hermana es alta. Mis amigos son simpáticos.*\n3. **Saying where someone is from.** *Es mexicana.* (She is Mexican.) *Son españoles.* (They are Spanish.) Nationalities take a small letter.\n4. **Giving an opinion with *muy*.** *La casa es muy bonita.* (The house is very pretty.) *Muy* never changes.\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| una casa blanca | a white house |\n| unos chicos simpáticos | some nice boys (or boys and girls) |\n| Las calles son muy estrechas. | The streets are very narrow. |\n| Mi profesora es colombiana. | My teacher is Colombian. |\n| Es un libro fácil. | It's an easy book. |\n| Los zapatos son azules. | The shoes are blue. |\n| Es un buen restaurante. | It's a good restaurant. |\n| Es un gran hombre. | He's a great man. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- An adjective can also stand after *ser*, far from the noun, and it still agrees: *La sopa es deliciosa. Los tomates son caros.*\n- Colors that are also nouns, like *naranja* (orange) and *rosa* (pink), usually do not change: *unas flores naranja*. Both *flores naranja* and *flores naranjas* are heard.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *la casa blanco* → ✓ *la casa blanca* — the adjective takes the gender of the noun.\n- ✗ *un rojo coche* → ✓ *un coche rojo* — colors and most adjectives come after the noun.\n- ✗ *las chicas simpáticos* → ✓ *las chicas simpáticas* — gender and number both agree.\n- ✗ *Ella es inglés.* → ✓ *Ella es inglesa.* — nationalities in a consonant have a feminine form.\n- ✗ *un bueno amigo* → ✓ *un buen amigo* — *bueno* loses the *-o* before a masculine singular noun.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the right form of the adjective.\n\n1. una ciudad (bonito)\n2. unos coches (rojo)\n3. unas mesas (grande)\n4. María es (español)\n\nPut the words in order.\n\n5. coche / un / azul\n6. amigo / un / buen\n7. simpáticas / las / chicas / son\n8. Translate: It's a white house.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. una ciudad bonita\n2. unos coches rojos\n3. unas mesas grandes\n4. María es española.\n5. un coche azul\n6. un buen amigo\n7. Las chicas son simpáticas.\n8. Es una casa blanca.\n","a1/grammar-alphabet-pronunciation.md":"---\nid: a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation\nlevel: A1\ncategory: grammar\ntitle: Alphabet, pronunciation and stress\nsummary: Spanish is read the way it is written. Learn the few letters that differ from English, where the stress falls, and ¿ ¡.\n---\n\n# Alphabet, pronunciation and stress\n\nSpanish spelling is regular: once you know the letters, you can read any word aloud and usually write a word you hear. Compare English *though*, *through* and *tough*. Spanish has five clear vowels, a few letters that differ from English, and simple stress rules.\n\n## How it works\n\n**Vowels.** There are five, and each has one short, clear sound. They never change into \"uh\" as in English *banana*.\n\n| Letter | Sounds like | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| a | *a* in *father* | *casa* (house) |\n| e | *e* in *bed* | *mesa* (table) |\n| i | *ee* in *see*, but short | *sí* (yes) |\n| o | *o* in *go*, but short, without the *w* glide at the end | *ocho* (eight) |\n| u | *oo* in *food*, short | *uno* (one) |\n\n**Letters that differ from English.**\n\n| Letter | How to say it | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **h** | silent | *hola*, *hotel* |\n| **j**, and **g** before e / i | a breathy sound from the throat, like a strong *h* | *José*, *gente* |\n| **ñ** | *ny* as in *canyon* | *España*, *mañana* |\n| **ll**, **y** | *y* as in *yes* (in some regions closer to *j* in *jam*; in Argentina and Uruguay like *sh*) | *calle*, *yo* |\n| **r** between vowels | one quick tap, like *tt* in American *better* | *pero* (but) |\n| **rr**, and **r** at the start of a word or after n, l, s | a rolled *r* | *perro* (dog), *rojo*, *Enrique* |\n| **b**, **v** | the same sound | *vaso*, *bar* |\n| **c** before e / i, **z** | *th* in *think* in most of Spain; *s* in Latin America, the Canary Islands and parts of southern Spain | *cine*, *zona* |\n| **c** before a / o / u, **g** before a / o / u | *k*; *g* in *go* | *casa*, *gracias* |\n| **qu** | *k* (the *u* is silent) | *queso* |\n\n**Stress.** Every word has one strong syllable. Three rules find it:\n\n1. A word ending in a **vowel, n or s** is stressed on the **second-to-last** syllable: *CA-sa*, *HA-blan*, *LU-nes*.\n2. A word ending in **any other consonant** is stressed on the **last** syllable: *ho-TEL*, *ciu-DAD*.\n3. A **written accent** breaks the rules and shows the stressed syllable: *ca-FÉ*, *te-LÉ-fo-no*, *Jo-SÉ*.\n\n**Questions and exclamations** have a mark at the start, upside down: *¿Cómo te llamas?* *¡Hola!*\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Reading a new word.** Say each letter, then put the stress where the rules say.\n2. **Spelling your name.** People will ask *¿Cómo se escribe?* (How do you spell it?). The letter names: a, be, ce, de, e, efe, ge, hache, i, jota, ka, ele, eme, ene, eñe, o, pe, cu, erre, ese, te, u, uve (Spain) / ve (Latin America), uve doble (Spain) / doble ve or doble u (Latin America), equis, ye, zeta.\n3. **Writing.** A missing accent or ñ can change the meaning: *papa* (potato, Latin America) and *papá* (dad); *ano* (anus) and *año* (year).\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| hola | hello (the h is silent) |\n| España | Spain |\n| la calle | the street |\n| el perro | the dog (rolled rr) |\n| pero | but (one tap) |\n| el teléfono | the telephone (te-LÉ-fo-no) |\n| ¿Cómo te llamas? | What's your name? |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *ch* sounds as in *church*: *chocolate*.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Como te llamas?* → ✓ *¿Cómo te llamas?* — a question needs ¿ at the start, and question words have an accent.\n- ✗ *Espana* → ✓ *España* — n and ñ are two different letters.\n- ✗ *pero* (for \"dog\") → ✓ *perro* — one r is a tap; rr is a roll, and it is a different word.\n- ✗ *Jose* → ✓ *José* — the name is stressed on the last syllable, and the accent shows it.\n\n## Practice\n\nWhere is the stress? Write the stressed syllable.\n\n1. mesa\n2. hotel\n3. café\n4. hablan\n\nWrite the word correctly.\n\n5. Espana (Spain)\n6. el pero (the dog)\n7. Como te llamas? (What's your name?)\n8. ola (hello)\n\n## Answers\n\n1. ME-sa (ends in a vowel)\n2. ho-TEL (ends in l)\n3. ca-FÉ (written accent)\n4. HA-blan (ends in n)\n5. España\n6. el perro\n7. ¿Cómo te llamas?\n8. hola\n","a1/grammar-articles.md":"---\nid: a1-grammar-articles\nlevel: A1\ncategory: grammar\ntitle: Definite and indefinite articles; al and del\nsummary: el, la, los, las and un, una, unos, unas; when Spanish uses an article where English does not, and a + el = al, de + el = del.\n---\n\n# Definite and indefinite articles; al and del\n\nSpanish articles work like English \"the\" and \"a\", with two differences. They change with the **gender** and **number** of the noun, so there are four of each. And Spanish uses \"the\" more often than English: for things in general (*la vida*, life), with titles (*el señor García*) and with days (*el lunes*). If your language has no articles at all, this is the topic to practice most.\n\n## How it works\n\n| | masculine | feminine |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **the** (singular) | **el** libro | **la** mesa |\n| **the** (plural) | **los** libros | **las** mesas |\n| **a / an** | **un** libro | **una** mesa |\n| **some** | **unos** libros | **unas** mesas |\n\n**Two short forms.** *A* (to) and *de* (of, from) join with *el*:\n\n- *a* + *el* = **al**: *el cine* → *Voy al cine.* (I'm going to the movies.)\n- *de* + *el* = **del**: *el profesor* → *el libro del profesor* (the teacher's book)\n- Only with *el*: *a la*, *de la*, *a los*, *de las* do not change.\n\n**A feminine noun that starts with a stressed a- takes *el* and *un*** in the singular, to avoid two *a* sounds: *el agua* (water), *un aula* (a classroom). It is still feminine: *el agua fría*, *las aguas*.\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Something specific, or already mentioned:** *el*, *la*, *los*, *las*. *Es la casa de Ana.* (It's Ana's house.)\n2. **Something new, one of many:** *un*, *una*. *Es un problema.* (It's a problem.)\n3. **Things in general** take *el / la / los / las*, where English uses no article: *El café es caro aquí.* (Coffee is expensive here.) *Los niños son curiosos.* (Children are curious.)\n4. **Talking about a person with a title:** *el señor García*, *la doctora Ruiz*. But not when you speak **to** the person: *Buenos días, señor García.*\n5. **Days of the week:** *el lunes* (on Monday), *los lunes* (on Mondays).\n6. **No article** with a job after *ser* (*Es médico*), with most countries (*Soy de Perú*), and for an unspecified amount of something: *Hay pan y leche.* (There's bread and milk.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Es un libro. Es el libro de Marta. | It's a book. It's Marta's book. |\n| Las mesas son nuevas. | The tables are new. |\n| El café es caro aquí. | Coffee is expensive here. |\n| El señor García es de Chile. | Mr. García is from Chile. |\n| Buenos días, señora Ruiz. | Good morning, Mrs. Ruiz. |\n| Voy al cine el sábado. | I'm going to the movies on Saturday. |\n| Es el coche del profesor. | It's the teacher's car. |\n| Son unos amigos de México. | They're friends from Mexico. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *Voy* (I'm going) is a form of *ir*; you will learn it later. Here it only shows *al*.\n- *De* + person shows possession, where English has *'s*: *la casa de Ana* (Ana's house).\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Coche es rojo.* → ✓ *El coche es rojo.* — a singular noun as the subject needs an article.\n- ✗ *Voy a el cine.* → ✓ *Voy al cine.* — *a* + *el* always joins into *al*.\n- ✗ *el libro de el profesor* → ✓ *el libro del profesor* — *de* + *el* joins into *del*.\n- ✗ *El señor García, ¿cómo está?* (speaking to him) → ✓ *Señor García, ¿cómo está?* — no article when you speak directly to someone.\n- ✗ *la agua* → ✓ *el agua* — a feminine noun with a stressed *a-* takes *el* in the singular.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite *el*, *la*, *los*, *las*, *un* or *una*.\n\n1. Es ___ casa muy bonita. (a)\n2. ___ niños son simpáticos. (the)\n3. ___ ciudad es grande. (the)\n4. Hay ___ problema. (a)\n\nWrite *al*, *del* or *de la*.\n\n5. Voy ___ cine. (el cine)\n6. Es el perro ___ vecino. (el vecino)\n7. Es la casa ___ profesora. (la profesora)\n8. Translate: Good afternoon, Mr. López.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. una\n2. Los\n3. La\n4. un\n5. al\n6. del\n7. de la\n8. Buenas tardes, señor López.\n","a1/grammar-gender-number.md":"---\nid: a1-grammar-gender-number\nlevel: A1\ncategory: grammar\ntitle: Gender and number of nouns\nsummary: Every Spanish noun is masculine or feminine. Learn the endings that tell you which, and how to make plurals.\n---\n\n# Gender and number of nouns\n\nIn English a table is just \"it\". In Spanish every noun is **masculine** or **feminine**, even a table or an idea: *el libro* (the book) is masculine, *la mesa* (the table) is feminine. The gender has nothing to do with the thing itself, but it matters because the words around the noun change with it. Learn every new noun together with *el* or *la*.\n\n## How it works\n\n**Gender: most nouns show it in the ending.**\n\n| Ending | Usually | Examples |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **-o** | masculine | *el libro*, *el vaso*, *el perro* |\n| **-a** | feminine | *la mesa*, *la casa*, *la playa* |\n| **-ción, -sión, -dad, -tad** | feminine | *la canción*, *la televisión*, *la ciudad*, *la libertad* |\n| **-or, -aje** | masculine | *el color*, *el viaje* |\n| **-e** or a consonant (other than -or, -aje) | either: learn it | *el coche*, *la noche*; *el hotel*, *la piel* |\n\nCommon exceptions: *el día*, *el mapa*, *el problema*, *el idioma*, *el sofá*; *la mano*, *la foto*, *la moto*, *la radio* (*el radio* for the device in Mexico and parts of Latin America), *la flor*.\n\n**People:** many nouns change the ending (*el amigo*, *la amiga*; *el profesor*, *la profesora*). Nouns in *-ista* and *-e* often keep one form: *el / la estudiante*, *el / la periodista*.\n\n**Number: making the plural.**\n\n| Singular ends in | Add | Examples |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| a vowel | **-s** | *libro* → *libros*, *coche* → *coches* |\n| a consonant | **-es** | *hotel* → *hoteles*, *ciudad* → *ciudades* |\n| **-z** | z → **-ces** | *lápiz* → *lápices*, *vez* → *veces* |\n\n- *el* → *los*, *la* → *las*: *los libros*, *las mesas*.\n- When a word stressed on the last syllable ends in *-n* or *-s* and adds *-es*, the written accent disappears: *canción* → *canciones*, *inglés* → *ingleses*. A stressed final vowel keeps it: *sofá* → *sofás*.\n- Words in unstressed *-s* do not change: *el lunes* → *los lunes*.\n- A group of men and women takes the masculine plural: *los alumnos* can be all boys or boys and girls.\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Every time you learn a noun.** Learn *la ciudad*, not just *ciudad*. The article is your note of the gender.\n2. **When you meet a new noun without an article.** Look at the ending and guess with the table; you will be right most of the time.\n3. **Talking about more than one.** *Dos hoteles*, *tres canciones*, *veinte días*.\n4. **Talking about mixed groups.** *Mis hermanos* (*mis* = my) can mean \"my brothers\" or \"my brothers and sisters\".\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el día y la noche | the day and the night |\n| la mano | the hand |\n| el problema | the problem |\n| las ciudades | the cities |\n| los lápices | the pencils |\n| dos canciones | two songs |\n| los estudiantes | the students (all men, or men and women) |\n| las estudiantes | the students (all women) |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- Some nouns change meaning with the gender: *el capital* (money) and *la capital* (the capital city).\n- Most countries and cities are used without an article: *Soy de Perú. Son de Madrid.*\n- Spanish has no neuter \"it\" nouns: everything is *el* or *la*.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *la problema* → ✓ *el problema* — *problema* ends in *-a* but is masculine.\n- ✗ *el mano* → ✓ *la mano* — *mano* ends in *-o* but is feminine.\n- ✗ *las ciudads* → ✓ *las ciudades* — after a consonant the plural adds *-es*.\n- ✗ *los lápizs* → ✓ *los lápices* — a final *z* becomes *c* before *-es*.\n\n## Practice\n\n*El* or *la*?\n\n1. ___ libro\n2. ___ canción\n3. ___ día\n4. ___ foto\n\nWrite the plural with the article.\n\n5. el hotel\n6. la ciudad\n7. el lápiz\n8. la canción\n\n## Answers\n\n1. el\n2. la\n3. el\n4. la\n5. los hoteles\n6. las ciudades\n7. los lápices\n8. las canciones\n","a1/grammar-possessives-demonstratives.md":"---\nid: a1-grammar-possessives-demonstratives\nlevel: A1\ncategory: grammar\ntitle: Possessives and demonstratives\nsummary: My, your, his, our (mi, tu, su, nuestro) and this, that, that over there (este, ese, aquel).\n---\n\n# Possessives and demonstratives\n\nYou have already used *mi* and *mis* in sentences like *Mi hermana es alta.* This page shows the whole set: *mi, tu, su, nuestro, vuestro*. They agree with the **thing owned**, not with the owner: *mis libros* (my books), *nuestra casa* (our house). It also shows the words for \"this\" and \"that\". Spanish has three distances instead of two: *este* (this, near me), *ese* (that, near you) and *aquel* (that, far from both of us).\n\n## How it works\n\n**Possessives** (before the noun):\n\n| Owner | one thing | several things |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | **mi** casa | **mis** libros |\n| tú | **tu** casa | **tus** libros |\n| él, ella, usted | **su** casa | **sus** libros |\n| nosotros, nosotras | **nuestro** coche / **nuestra** casa | **nuestros** amigos / **nuestras** amigas |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | **vuestro** coche / **vuestra** casa | **vuestros** amigos / **vuestras** amigas |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | **su** casa | **sus** libros |\n\n- *Mi, tu, su* change only for the plural (*mis, tus, sus*). *Nuestro* and *vuestro* also change for the feminine.\n- *Tu* (your) has no accent; *tú* (you) has one.\n- *Su* can mean his, her, its, your (formal) or their. If it is unclear, use *de* + the person: *la casa de Ana*, *el coche de ustedes*.\n\n**Demonstratives** (they agree with the noun):\n\n| | near me | near you | far from both |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| masculine | **este** libro | **ese** libro | **aquel** libro |\n| feminine | **esta** mesa | **esa** mesa | **aquella** mesa |\n| masculine plural | **estos** libros | **esos** libros | **aquellos** libros |\n| feminine plural | **estas** mesas | **esas** mesas | **aquellas** mesas |\n\n- Without a noun they mean \"this one\", \"that one\": *Quiero este.* (I want this one.)\n- *Esto*, *eso* (and *aquello*, far away) refer to something unknown or an idea: *¿Qué es esto?* (What is this?) *Eso es verdad.* (That's true.)\n- *Aquí* (here), *ahí* (there, near you) and *allí* (over there) match the three distances.\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Talking about your things and people.** *Mi casa es pequeña. Mis padres viven en Lima.*\n2. **Asking about someone else's.** *¿Cómo se llama tu perro? ¿Dónde está su hotel, señora?*\n3. **Pointing in a shop or a café.** *¿Cuánto cuesta esta camisa? Quiero ese pastel.*\n4. **Something far away.** *¿Ves aquella casa? Es de mis abuelos.* (Do you see that house? It's my grandparents'.)\n5. **An unknown thing or an idea.** *¿Qué es eso?* (What's that?)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Mi madre es profesora y mis hermanos son estudiantes. | My mother is a teacher and my brothers and sisters are students. |\n| ¿Es tu coche? | Is it your car? |\n| Nuestra casa está cerca del centro. | Our house is near downtown. |\n| Señor López, ¿es su maleta? | Mr. López, is it your suitcase? |\n| Este café está muy bueno. | This coffee is very good. |\n| ¿Te gustan esas fotos? | Do you like those photos (near you)? |\n| Aquellos edificios son nuevos. | Those buildings (over there) are new. |\n| ¿Qué es esto? | What's this? |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- With body parts and clothes, Spanish often uses *el / la* instead of *mi*: *Me lavo las manos.*\n- Texts written before 2010 often have an accent on *éste*, *ése*; today they are written without it.\n- In Latin America *aquel* is used less in speech; *ese* often covers both distances.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *mi libros* → ✓ *mis libros* — the possessive agrees with the things, which are plural.\n- ✗ *nuestro casa* → ✓ *nuestra casa* — *nuestro* agrees with the feminine *casa*.\n- ✗ *Esto libro es bueno.* → ✓ *Este libro es bueno.* — before a noun, use *este*, not *esto*.\n- ✗ *la mi casa* → ✓ *mi casa* — no article before *mi, tu, su*.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the possessive.\n\n1. (yo) ___ amigos\n2. (nosotros) ___ casa\n3. (tú) ___ hermana\n4. (ellos) ___ padres\n\nWrite the right form of *este* or *ese*.\n\n5. ___ (this) mesa\n6. ___ (these) libros\n7. ___ (that, near you) chaqueta\n8. Translate: What's that?\n\n## Answers\n\n1. mis amigos\n2. nuestra casa\n3. tu hermana\n4. sus padres\n5. esta mesa\n6. estos libros\n7. esa chaqueta\n8. ¿Qué es eso?\n","a1/grammar-prepositions-place-time.md":"---\nid: a1-grammar-prepositions-place-time\nlevel: A1\ncategory: grammar\ntitle: Basic prepositions of place and time\nsummary: en, a, de, con, entre, desde, hasta, para; with places, times, days, months and seasons.\n---\n\n# Basic prepositions of place and time\n\nA few short words do most of the work: *en*, *a*, *de*, *con*, and they do not match English one to one. *En* covers \"in\", \"on\" and \"at\" for places (*en casa*, *en la mesa*), but \"at\" a clock time is *a* (*a las tres*). And with days Spanish uses no preposition at all: *el lunes* means \"on Monday\". Learn them in phrases, not as single words.\n\n## How it works\n\n**Place**\n\n| Spanish | English | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **en** | in, on, at (a place) | *en casa*, *en la mesa*, *en el trabajo* |\n| **a** | to (movement) | *Voy a Madrid. Voy al banco.* |\n| **de** | from, of | *Soy de Perú. el libro de Ana* |\n| **desde … hasta** | from … to | *desde Lima hasta Cusco* |\n| **entre** | between | *entre el banco y la farmacia* |\n| **con** / **sin** | with / without | *café con leche*, *sin azúcar* |\n| **para** | for, to (a purpose, a person) | *un regalo para ti* (a present for you) |\n\nLocation phrases with *de*: *cerca de*, *lejos de*, *al lado de*, *enfrente de* (opposite), *delante de* (in front of), *detrás de* (behind), *encima de* (on top of), *debajo de* (under). Remember *de* + *el* = *del*.\n\n**Time**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| **a** las tres | at three |\n| **por** la mañana / tarde / noche (Latin America also: **en** la mañana) | in the morning / afternoon / at night |\n| **el** lunes / **los** lunes | on Monday / on Mondays |\n| **en** julio, **en** verano, **en** 2026 | in July, in summer, in 2026 |\n| **de** nueve **a** cinco | from nine to five |\n| **desde** las ocho / **hasta** las ocho | from (since) eight / until eight |\n| **antes de** / **después de** comer | before / after eating |\n\n- After a preposition (but not *entre*: *entre tú y yo*), the pronouns *yo* and *tú* become **mí** and **ti**: *para mí*, *para ti*. With *con*: **conmigo**, **contigo**.\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Where something is:** *en*. *Las llaves están en la mesa.* (The keys are on the table.)\n2. **Where you go:** *a*. *Vamos a la playa.* (We're going to the beach.)\n3. **Where something comes from:** *de*. *El tren de Sevilla llega a las diez.*\n4. **When:** *a las*, *por la*, *el*, *en*. *Trabajo por la mañana. En agosto no trabajo.*\n5. **Who with or for:** *con*, *para*. *¿Vienes conmigo? Este regalo es para ti.*\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Estoy en el trabajo. | I'm at work. |\n| Voy a la oficina en autobús. | I go to the office by bus. |\n| La clase es de diez a doce. | The class is from ten to twelve. |\n| Nos vemos el sábado por la tarde. | See you on Saturday afternoon. |\n| En invierno hace frío. | It's cold in winter. |\n| El gato está debajo de la cama. | The cat is under the bed. |\n| ¿Quieres venir conmigo? | Do you want to come with me? |\n| Me lavo los dientes después de comer. | I brush my teeth after eating. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- Means of transport take *en*: *en coche, en tren, en avión*. But \"on foot\" is *a pie* or *caminando*.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *en el lunes* → ✓ *el lunes* — no preposition with days.\n- ✗ *en las tres* → ✓ *a las tres* — \"at\" a clock time is *a*.\n- ✗ *Voy en la playa.* → ✓ *Voy a la playa.* — movement to a place takes *a*.\n- ✗ *con yo* → ✓ *conmigo* — \"with me\" is one word.\n- ✗ *para tú* → ✓ *para ti* — after a preposition *tú* becomes *ti*.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the preposition or the article.\n\n1. Vivo ___ Madrid.\n2. Voy ___ la universidad.\n3. La tienda abre ___ las nueve.\n4. Trabajo ___ la mañana.\n5. Mi cumpleaños es ___ mayo.\n6. Nos vemos ___ viernes.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n7. with me\n8. a coffee with milk and no sugar\n\n## Answers\n\n1. en\n2. a\n3. a\n4. por\n5. en\n6. el (no preposition, but the article: nos vemos el viernes)\n7. conmigo\n8. un café con leche sin azúcar / un café con leche y sin azúcar\n","a1/grammar-questions-negation.md":"---\nid: a1-grammar-questions-negation\nlevel: A1\ncategory: grammar\ntitle: Question words and negation\nsummary: Ask with qué, quién, dónde, cuándo, cómo, cuánto, por qué; say no with no before the verb, and nunca, nada, nadie.\n---\n\n# Question words and negation\n\nSpanish questions are simpler than English ones: there is no \"do\" or \"does\". You keep the normal verb and add ¿ ? and a rising voice, or start with a question word. Question words carry a **written accent** when they ask (or exclaim). Negation is simple too: put **no** before the verb. One surprise for English speakers: Spanish uses a double negative, *No como nada* (\"I don't eat nothing\"), and it is correct.\n\n## How it works\n\n**Yes / no questions:** the same words as a statement, with ¿ ? Often the subject goes after the verb.\n\n- *Hablas español.* → *¿Hablas español?* (Do you speak Spanish?)\n- *¿Trabaja Ana aquí?* / *¿Ana trabaja aquí?* (Does Ana work here?) Both orders are correct.\n\n**Question words** (always with an accent):\n\n| Spanish | English | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ¿qué? | what? | *¿Qué comes?* |\n| ¿quién? / ¿quiénes? | who? | *¿Quién es?* |\n| ¿dónde? | where? | *¿Dónde vives?* |\n| ¿de dónde? | where … from? | *¿De dónde eres?* |\n| ¿cuándo? | when? | *¿Cuándo trabajas?* |\n| ¿cómo? | how? / what … like? | *¿Cómo es tu casa?* |\n| ¿cuánto? / ¿cuánta? / ¿cuántos? / ¿cuántas? | how much? / how many? | *¿Cuántos años tienes?* |\n| ¿cuál? / ¿cuáles? | which? / what? | *¿Cuál es tu número?* |\n| ¿por qué? | why? | *¿Por qué estudias español?* |\n\n- The question word comes first; the verb comes right after it: *¿Dónde trabaja tu madre?*\n- A preposition goes before the question word: *¿De dónde eres? ¿Con quién vives?*\n- *Cuánto* agrees with the noun: *¿Cuántas hermanas tienes?*\n- The answer to *¿por qué?* starts with *porque* (one word, no accent): *Porque me gusta.* (Because I like it.)\n\n**Negation:**\n\n- *no* + verb: *No hablo francés.* (I don't speak French.)\n- Short answers: *—¿Eres de aquí? —No, no soy de aquí.* The first *no* answers, the second one negates the verb.\n- **Negative words** need *no* before the verb when they come after it: *No como nunca carne.* (I never eat meat.) *No tengo nada.* (I have nothing.) *No hay nadie.* (There is nobody.) Before the verb, they work alone: *Nunca como carne.*\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Asking for information.** *¿Dónde está el baño?* (Where is the bathroom?)\n2. **Checking something.** *¿Trabajas mañana?* (Are you working tomorrow?)\n3. **Saying what you don't do or have.** *No tengo perro.* (I don't have a dog.)\n4. **Saying \"never\", \"nothing\", \"nobody\".** *No trabajo nunca los domingos.* (I never work on Sundays.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Qué estudias? | What do you study? |\n| ¿Quién es la chica de la foto? | Who is the girl in the photo? |\n| ¿Cuándo llega el tren? | When does the train arrive? |\n| ¿Cuántos hermanos tienes? | How many brothers and sisters do you have? |\n| ¿Por qué no comes? —Porque no tengo hambre. | Why aren't you eating? —Because I'm not hungry. |\n| No vivo aquí. | I don't live here. |\n| No como nunca pescado. | I never eat fish. |\n| No hay nadie en casa. | There's nobody at home. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *¿Qué?* or *¿Cómo?* can mean \"Sorry? What did you say?\". *¿Cómo?* is more polite.\n- *¿Qué es…?* asks for a definition (*¿Qué es una tortilla?*); *¿Cuál es…?* asks for one item of information (*¿Cuál es tu dirección?*).\n- In exclamations the accent stays: *¡Qué bonito!* (How pretty!)\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *¿Donde vives?* → ✓ *¿Dónde vives?* — question words carry a written accent.\n- ✗ *No veo algo.* → ✓ *No veo nada.* — after *no*, use the negative word *nada*.\n- ✗ *Hablo no inglés.* → ✓ *No hablo inglés.* — *no* goes right before the verb.\n- ✗ *¿Por qué? —Por qué no tengo tiempo.* → ✓ *—Porque no tengo tiempo.* — the answer uses *porque*, one word.\n\n## Practice\n\nChoose the question word.\n\n1. ¿___ vives? —En Lima. (Dónde / Cuándo)\n2. ¿___ años tienes? —Veinte. (Cuántos / Cuántas)\n3. ¿___ es tu profesor? —Es simpático. (Cómo / Qué)\n4. ¿___ estudias español? —Porque me gusta. (Por qué / Porque)\n\nMake the sentences negative.\n\n5. Hablo alemán.\n6. Tengo algo. (→ nada)\n7. Hay alguien en casa. (→ nadie)\n8. Translate: I never work on Sundays.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. Dónde\n2. Cuántos\n3. Cómo\n4. Por qué\n5. No hablo alemán.\n6. No tengo nada.\n7. No hay nadie en casa.\n8. Nunca trabajo los domingos. / No trabajo nunca los domingos.\n","a1/grammar-subject-pronouns.md":"---\nid: a1-grammar-subject-pronouns\nlevel: A1\ncategory: grammar\ntitle: Subject pronouns; tú vs usted\nsummary: I, you, he, she, we, they in Spanish; four words for \"you\"; and why Spanish often leaves the pronoun out.\n---\n\n# Subject pronouns; tú vs usted\n\nSubject pronouns are the words for \"I\", \"you\", \"she\" and so on. Spanish has more of them than English, for two reasons. First, \"you\" has a friendly form and a polite form, and a singular and a plural. Second, \"we\" and \"they\" show whether the group is all female. But Spanish uses them much less, because the verb ending already shows who is meant.\n\n## How it works\n\n| English | Spanish | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| I | **yo** | no capital letter |\n| you (one friend) | **tú** | with an accent; *tu* without it means \"your\" |\n| he / she | **él** / **ella** | *él* with an accent; *el* without it means \"the\" |\n| you (one person, formal) | **usted** | often written *Ud.* |\n| we | **nosotros** / **nosotras** | *nosotras*: only women |\n| you (a group of friends) (Spain) | **vosotros** / **vosotras** | *vosotras*: only women |\n| they | **ellos** / **ellas** | *ellas*: only women |\n| you (a group) | **ustedes** | formal in Spain; all groups in Latin America. Often *Uds.* |\n\n- A mixed group takes the masculine form: Ana and Pedro are *ellos*; Ana, Rosa and Pedro are still *ellos*.\n- **Usted** and **ustedes** mean \"you\", but they take the same verb form as *él* and *ellos*: *¿Cómo está usted?* is \"How are you?\"\n- There is **no word for \"it\"** as a subject: *Es grande.* (It is big.)\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Choose tú or usted.** *Tú* for friends, family, children and people your age in relaxed settings. *Usted* for older people, customers, officials and anyone you would call \"sir\" or \"madam\". To a friend: *¿Y tú?* To your new boss: *¿Y usted?*\n2. **Talk to a group.** In Spain, *vosotros* for friends and *ustedes* for formal groups. In Latin America, *ustedes* for every group. Both are correct; use the one people use where you are.\n3. **Leave the pronoun out.** In a normal sentence the verb is enough: *Me llamo Ana* (not *Yo me llamo Ana* every time).\n4. **Use the pronoun for contrast or emphasis.** *Yo me llamo Ana y él se llama Pedro.* (I'm Ana, and **he** is Pedro.) *—¿Quién es Marta? —Yo.* (Me.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| —Bien, gracias. ¿Y tú? | Fine, thanks. And you? |\n| —Muy bien. ¿Y usted, señora? | Very well. And you, madam? |\n| Él se llama Luis y ella se llama Rosa. | He's called Luis and she's called Rosa. |\n| ¿Cómo se llama usted? | What's your name? (formal) |\n| —¿Y vosotros? —Nosotros, bien. (Spain) | \"And you guys?\" \"We're fine.\" |\n| —¿Y ustedes? —Nosotras, muy bien. | \"And you?\" \"We're very well.\" (a group of women) |\n| Mucho gusto. Yo soy Pablo. | Nice to meet you. I'm Pablo. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- In Argentina, Uruguay, Paraguay and much of Central America (and in parts of other countries) people say *vos* instead of *tú* with different verb forms (*vos sos*, *vos tenés*). You will understand it from context; this course uses *tú*.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Yo me llamo Ana. Yo soy de Lima. Yo soy profesora.* → ✓ *Me llamo Ana. Soy de Lima. Soy profesora.* — repeating *yo* in every sentence sounds unnatural.\n- ✗ *Usted te llamas Pedro.* → ✓ *Usted se llama Pedro.* — *usted* takes the forms of *él*, not of *tú*.\n- ✗ *Ellos* (for Ana and María) → ✓ *Ellas* — a group of only women is *ellas*, and *nosotras* for \"we\".\n- ✗ *Él es grande.* (about a table) → ✓ *Es grande.* — there is no subject pronoun for \"it\".\n- ✗ *tu* (for \"you\") → ✓ *tú* — without the accent, *tu* means \"your\".\n\n## Practice\n\nWhich pronoun? Write it in Spanish.\n\n1. you, to your best friend\n2. you, to an old man you meet in a museum\n3. they (Elena and Sofía)\n4. we (you and your brother; you are a woman)\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. And you? (to a group of friends in Mexico)\n6. And you? (to a group of friends in Madrid)\n7. He's called Pablo.\n8. What's your name? (to a hotel receptionist)\n\n## Answers\n\n1. tú\n2. usted\n3. ellas\n4. nosotros\n5. ¿Y ustedes?\n6. ¿Y vosotros? / ¿Y vosotras? (¿Y ustedes? is also understood)\n7. Se llama Pablo. / Él se llama Pablo.\n8. ¿Cómo se llama usted? / ¿Cómo se llama?\n","a1/verbs-estar-hay.md":"---\nid: a1-verbs-estar-hay\nlevel: A1\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: estar for location and states; hay vs está\nsummary: Estar says where something is and how someone feels. Hay says that something exists; está says where a known thing is.\n---\n\n# estar for location and states; hay vs está\n\n*Estar* is the second Spanish verb for \"to be\". You use it for **where** a person or thing is (*Estoy en casa*) and for **how** someone or something is at the moment (*Estoy cansado*). Next to it stands *hay*, \"there is / there are\", which says that something **exists** somewhere: *Hay un banco en la plaza.* English uses \"to be\" for all of this, so these three are worth learning together.\n\n## How it works\n\n| Person | estar | English |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | **estoy** | I am |\n| tú | **estás** | you are |\n| él, ella, usted | **está** | he / she is, you are (formal) |\n| nosotros, nosotras | **estamos** | we are |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | **estáis** | you are (plural) |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | **están** | they are, you are (plural) |\n\n- Watch the accents: *estás*, *está*, *estáis*, *están*. *Esta* without an accent means \"this\".\n- **Location:** *estar* + *en* / *aquí* / *allí* …: *Estamos en el hotel.*\n- **States and feelings:** *estar* + adjective, which agrees: *Ana está cansada. Los niños están contentos.*\n\n**Hay** (from the verb *haber*) has one form for singular and plural:\n\n- *Hay* + **a / an, numbers, or no article**: *Hay un parque. Hay dos bancos. Hay pan.*\n- *Está / están* + **the, a name or my**: *El parque está allí. Mis llaves están en la mesa.*\n\nAsk with *¿Hay…?* when you don't know if something exists, and with *¿Dónde está…?* when you know it does: *¿Hay un banco por aquí?* (Is there a bank around here?) *¿Dónde está el banco?* (Where is the bank?)\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Where someone or something is.** *¿Dónde estás? —Estoy en el trabajo.* (I'm at work.)\n2. **How someone feels now.** *Estoy cansado.* (I'm tired.) *¿Estás bien?* (Are you OK?)\n3. **The state of something.** *La puerta está abierta.* (The door is open.) *El café está frío.* (The coffee is cold.)\n4. **Asking and saying how someone is.** *¿Cómo estás? —Muy bien, gracias.*\n5. **Saying what there is in a place:** *hay*. *En mi calle hay dos restaurantes.* (There are two restaurants on my street.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Estoy en casa. | I'm at home. |\n| ¿Dónde están las llaves? | Where are the keys? |\n| Marta está enferma hoy. | Marta is sick today. |\n| Estamos muy contentos. | We're very happy. |\n| La tienda está cerrada. | The store is closed. |\n| Hay un supermercado en la esquina. | There's a supermarket on the corner. |\n| ¿Hay wifi en el hotel? | Is there wifi in the hotel? |\n| No hay leche. | There's no milk. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *Hay* has no plural form: *Hay una farmacia. Hay tres farmacias.* Never *hayn*.\n- *Estar* with *bien* / *mal*: *Estoy bien.* (I'm fine.) *Bien* never changes.\n- The difference between *ser* and *estar* with adjectives (*es aburrido* / *está aburrido*) comes in [[a1-verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics]].\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Soy en casa.* → ✓ *Estoy en casa.* — where you are takes *estar*.\n- ✗ *Hay el banco en la plaza.* → ✓ *El banco está en la plaza.* — with *el*, *la* use *está*; *hay* is for something new (*hay un banco*).\n- ✗ *Están dos farmacias en mi calle.* → ✓ *Hay dos farmacias en mi calle.* — \"there are\" is *hay*.\n- ✗ *Ella esta cansada.* → ✓ *Ella está cansada.* — *esta* without the accent means \"this\".\n- ✗ *Estoy cansado* (said by a woman) → ✓ *Estoy cansada.* — the adjective agrees with the speaker.\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete with the right form of *estar*.\n\n1. Yo ___ en la oficina.\n2. ¿Dónde ___ (tú)?\n3. Los niños ___ cansados.\n\n*Hay* or *está / están*?\n\n4. ___ un restaurante muy bueno cerca.\n5. El restaurante ___ en la calle Mayor.\n6. ¿Dónde ___ mis llaves?\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n7. Is there a pharmacy around here?\n8. We're at home.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. estoy\n2. estás\n3. están\n4. Hay\n5. está\n6. están\n7. ¿Hay una farmacia por aquí?\n8. Estamos en casa.\n","a1/verbs-gustar.md":"---\nid: a1-verbs-gustar\nlevel: A1\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: gustar and similar verbs\nsummary: Say what you like and don't like. In Spanish the thing you like is the subject, not you.\n---\n\n# gustar and similar verbs\n\nIn English you say \"I like coffee\", and **I** is the subject. Spanish turns the sentence around: *Me gusta el café* is, word for word, \"Coffee pleases me\". The thing you like is the subject; you are the person it pleases. That is why you only need two forms of the verb: *gusta* and *gustan*.\n\n## How it works\n\n**(a + person) + pronoun + gusta / gustan + what you like**\n\n| Who likes it | Pronoun | One thing, or an action | Several things |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| (a mí) | **me** | gusta | gustan |\n| (a ti) | **te** | gusta | gustan |\n| (a él, a ella, a usted) | **le** | gusta | gustan |\n| (a nosotros, a nosotras) | **nos** | gusta | gustan |\n| (a vosotros, a vosotras) (Spain) | **os** | gusta | gustan |\n| (a ellos, a ellas, a ustedes) | **les** | gusta | gustan |\n\n- *gusta* + singular noun **with its article**: *Me gusta **el** café.*\n- *gusta* + infinitive, also with two or more infinitives: *Me gusta bailar. Me gusta cantar y bailar.*\n- *gustan* + plural noun: *Me gustan **los** gatos.*\n- Negative: *no* goes before the pronoun: *No me gusta el té.*\n- Questions keep the same word order: *¿Te gusta el fútbol?*\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Say what you like or don't like.** *Me gusta la música.* (I like music.) *No me gustan los lunes.* (I don't like Mondays.)\n2. **Ask someone.** To a friend: *¿Te gusta cocinar?* Formal: *¿Le gusta la habitación?*\n3. **Say how much.** *Me gusta mucho el cine.* (I like movies a lot.) *No me gusta nada el frío.* (I don't like the cold at all.) *Me encanta* means \"I love\": *Me encanta esta ciudad.*\n4. **Make clear who.** *Le* can mean he, she or you (formal), so add *a* + the person: *A María le gusta el cine. A mí no.*\n5. **Agree or disagree.** *—Me gusta el café. —A mí también.* (Me too.) *—No me gusta el té. —A mí tampoco.* (Me neither.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Me gusta el chocolate. | I like chocolate. |\n| ¿Te gustan los perros? | Do you like dogs? |\n| No nos gusta trabajar los sábados. | We don't like working on Saturdays. |\n| A Carlos le gusta mucho viajar. | Carlos really likes traveling. |\n| A mis padres les gustan las películas antiguas. | My parents like old movies. |\n| Me encanta esta ciudad. | I love this city. |\n| ¿Os gusta la comida mexicana? (Spain) | Do you all like Mexican food? |\n| —No me gusta el frío. —A mí tampoco. | \"I don't like the cold.\" \"Me neither.\" |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- The same pattern works for *encantar* (to love), *interesar* (to interest) and *doler* (to hurt): *Me interesan los idiomas. Me duele la cabeza.*\n- *Me encanta* is already strong. Do not add *mucho*.\n- About people, *Me gustas* means \"I am attracted to you\". To say you like someone as a person, use *Me cae bien*: *Tu hermano me cae muy bien.*\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Yo gusto el café.* → ✓ *Me gusta el café.* — you are not the subject; the coffee is.\n- ✗ *Me gusta los gatos.* → ✓ *Me gustan los gatos.* — the verb agrees with the thing you like.\n- ✗ *Me gusta café.* → ✓ *Me gusta el café.* — things you like in general need the article.\n- ✗ *María le gusta bailar.* → ✓ *A María le gusta bailar.* — the person needs *a*.\n- ✗ *Me gusto bailar.* → ✓ *Me gusta bailar.* — *me gusto* means \"I like myself\".\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete with the pronoun and *gusta* or *gustan*.\n\n1. (a mí) ___ ___ la paella.\n2. (a ti) ¿___ ___ los deportes?\n3. A Ana ___ ___ leer.\n4. (a nosotros) No ___ ___ las verduras.\n5. A mis amigos ___ ___ cantar y bailar.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n6. I don't like coffee.\n7. Do you like the hotel? (formal)\n8. \"I love dogs.\" \"Me too.\"\n\n## Answers\n\n1. Me gusta\n2. Te gustan\n3. le gusta\n4. nos gustan\n5. les gusta\n6. No me gusta el café.\n7. ¿Le gusta el hotel?\n8. —Me encantan los perros. —A mí también.\n","a1/verbs-ir-a-infinitive.md":"---\nid: a1-verbs-ir-a-infinitive\nlevel: A1\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: ir a + infinitive: plans\nsummary: Voy a visitar a mi madre. Talk about plans and intentions with ir a + an infinitive, like English \"going to\".\n---\n\n# ir a + infinitive: plans\n\nYou already know *ir* (to go): *voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van*. Put *a* and an infinitive after it and you have the easiest way to talk about the future: *Voy a estudiar* (I'm going to study). It works just like English \"going to\", so it is the first future form most learners use. *Vamos a* + infinitive can also mean \"let's\": *Vamos a ver.* (Let's see.)\n\n## How it works\n\n**ir (present) + a + infinitive**\n\n| Person | ir a + infinitive | English |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | **voy a** comer | I'm going to eat |\n| tú | **vas a** comer | you're going to eat |\n| él, ella, usted | **va a** comer | he / she is going to eat, you're going to eat |\n| nosotros, nosotras | **vamos a** comer | we're going to eat |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | **vais a** comer | you're going to eat |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | **van a** comer | they're going to eat, you're going to eat |\n\n- Only *ir* changes; *a* and the infinitive stay the same.\n- **Negative:** *No voy a salir.* (I'm not going to go out.)\n- **Question:** *¿Qué vas a hacer mañana?* (What are you going to do tomorrow?)\n- **Reflexive verbs:** the pronoun goes before *ir* or joins the infinitive: *Me voy a acostar.* = *Voy a acostarme.*\n\n**Time words for the future:** *mañana* (tomorrow), *pasado mañana* (the day after tomorrow), *esta tarde* (this afternoon), *esta noche* (tonight), *este fin de semana* (this weekend), *la semana que viene* / *la próxima semana* (next week), *el año que viene* (next year).\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Plans.** *Este verano voy a viajar a México.* (This summer I'm going to travel to Mexico.)\n2. **Intentions.** *Voy a estudiar más.* (I'm going to study more.)\n3. **Something you can see is about to happen.** *¡Cuidado! Te vas a caer.* (Careful! You're going to fall.)\n4. **Suggestions with *vamos a*.** *Vamos a empezar.* (Let's start.)\n5. **Asking about plans.** *¿Qué vais a hacer el sábado?* (Spain) / *¿Qué van a hacer el sábado?*\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Voy a visitar a mi madre. | I'm going to visit my mother. |\n| ¿Qué vas a hacer esta noche? | What are you going to do tonight? |\n| Mañana vamos a comer en un restaurante. | Tomorrow we're going to eat in a restaurant. |\n| Mis amigos van a venir a las ocho. | My friends are going to come at eight. |\n| No voy a trabajar el lunes. | I'm not going to work on Monday. |\n| Va a llover. | It's going to rain. |\n| Vamos a ver. | Let's see. |\n| ¿Vas a venir a la fiesta? | Are you going to come to the party? |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- When the object of the verb is a person, add *a*: *Voy a visitar **a** mi madre*, *llamar a Ana* (but *visitar Roma*). This \"personal a\" has no English equivalent.\n- *Voy a la playa* (I'm going to the beach) is *ir a* + a place, not a plan with an infinitive. Both use *a*.\n- The present can also talk about fixed plans: *Mañana trabajo.* *Ir a* puts the intention first. See the card *ir a + infinitivo* in the Tense & Mood Lab.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Voy comer.* → ✓ *Voy a comer.* — do not leave out *a*.\n- ✗ *Vamos a salimos.* → ✓ *Vamos a salir.* — after *a* comes the infinitive.\n- ✗ *Yo va a estudiar.* → ✓ *Yo voy a estudiar.* — *ir* changes with the person.\n- ✗ *Voy a visitar mi madre.* → ✓ *Voy a visitar a mi madre.* — a person after *visitar* takes *a*.\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete with *ir a* + infinitive.\n\n1. Yo ___ (estudiar) esta tarde.\n2. ¿Tú ___ (venir) mañana?\n3. Nosotros ___ (comer) en casa.\n4. Ellos no ___ (trabajar) el sábado.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. What are you going to do tomorrow? (to a friend)\n6. I'm going to call my sister.\n7. It's going to rain.\n8. Let's start.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. voy a estudiar\n2. vas a venir\n3. vamos a comer\n4. van a trabajar\n5. ¿Qué vas a hacer mañana?\n6. Voy a llamar a mi hermana.\n7. Va a llover.\n8. Vamos a empezar.\n","a1/verbs-present-irregular.md":"---\nid: a1-verbs-present-irregular\nlevel: A1\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: Common irregular and stem-changing verbs in the present\nsummary: The verbs you use most are irregular: ir, hacer, querer, poder, salir, jugar. Learn the yo forms and the three stem changes.\n---\n\n# Common irregular and stem-changing verbs in the present\n\nThe most useful Spanish verbs are irregular, as in English (*be*, *have*, *go*), but they follow a few patterns. Some have an odd **yo** form (*hago*, *salgo*). Others change a vowel in the **stem**: *querer* → *quiero*, *poder* → *puedo*, *pedir* → *pido*. And a few, like *ir* (to go), you simply learn by heart.\n\n## How it works\n\n**1. Stem changes** (in every form except *nosotros* and *vosotros*):\n\n| e → ie: querer (to want) | o → ue: poder (can) | e → i: pedir (to ask for) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| qu**ie**ro | p**ue**do | p**i**do |\n| qu**ie**res | p**ue**des | p**i**des |\n| qu**ie**re | p**ue**de | p**i**de |\n| queremos | podemos | pedimos |\n| queréis | podéis | pedís |\n| qu**ie**ren | p**ue**den | p**i**den |\n\nMore: e → ie: *empezar* (to start), *pensar* (to think), *preferir* (to prefer), *cerrar* (to close). o → ue: *dormir* (to sleep), *volver* (to come back), *costar* (to cost), *encontrar* (to find). e → i: *repetir* (to repeat), *servir* (to serve). **u → ue:** only *jugar* (to play): *juego, juegas, juega, jugamos, jugáis, juegan*.\n\n**2. Irregular yo form, the rest regular:**\n\n| Verb | yo | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| hacer (to do, to make) | **hago** | *Hago la cena.* |\n| salir (to go out, to leave) | **salgo** | *Salgo a las ocho.* |\n| poner (to put) | **pongo** | *Pongo la mesa.* |\n| traer (to bring) | **traigo** | *Traigo vino.* |\n| conocer (to know a person or place) | **conozco** | *Conozco Madrid.* |\n| saber (to know a fact) | **sé** | *No sé.* |\n| ver (to see) | **veo** | *Veo la tele.* |\n\n**3. Fully irregular: ir (to go):** *voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van*. Usually with *a* (remember *a* + *el* = *al*): *Voy a la playa. Vamos al cine.*\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Saying what you want and can do.** *Quiero un café. ¿Puedes venir mañana?* (Can you come tomorrow?)\n2. **Talking about your week.** *Los sábados juego al fútbol y salgo con amigos.*\n3. **Saying where you go.** *Voy al trabajo en autobús.* (I go to work by bus.)\n4. **Asking for things.** *Pido una pizza.* (I order a pizza.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Quieres un té? | Would you like a cup of tea? |\n| No puedo, trabajo mañana. | I can't, I'm working tomorrow. |\n| ¿A qué hora empieza la clase? | What time does the class start? |\n| Los domingos duermo mucho. | On Sundays I sleep a lot. |\n| ¿Cuánto cuesta? | How much is it? |\n| Mis hijos juegan al tenis. | My kids play tennis. |\n| Hago deporte dos veces a la semana. | I exercise twice a week. |\n| Vamos a la playa en agosto. | We go to the beach in August. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *Jugar a* + game: *jugar al fútbol*, *jugar a las cartas*. In parts of Latin America people also say *jugar fútbol*.\n- *Saber* is for facts and skills (*Sé nadar*, I can swim); *conocer* is for people and places (*Conozco a Ana*). Before a person, *conocer* takes *a*.\n- *Pedir* is \"to ask for\" something; *preguntar* is \"to ask\" a question.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Yo quero un café.* → ✓ *Yo quiero un café.* — *querer* changes e to ie in the stem.\n- ✗ *Nosotros quieremos.* → ✓ *Nosotros queremos.* — no stem change in *nosotros* and *vosotros*.\n- ✗ *Yo hace la cena.* → ✓ *Yo hago la cena.* — the *yo* form of *hacer* is *hago*.\n- ✗ *Yo sabo.* → ✓ *Yo sé.* — the *yo* form of *saber* is *sé*.\n- ✗ *Conozco que es tarde.* → ✓ *Sé que es tarde.* — facts take *saber*; *conocer* is for people and places.\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete with the right form.\n\n1. Yo no ___ (poder) venir hoy.\n2. ¿Tú ___ (querer) un café?\n3. Nosotros ___ (dormir) ocho horas.\n4. Yo ___ (hacer) la cena.\n5. Ellos ___ (ir) a la playa.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n6. I'm going out with my friends. (salir)\n7. What time does the movie start? (empezar)\n8. I don't know.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. puedo\n2. quieres\n3. dormimos\n4. hago\n5. van\n6. Salgo con mis amigos.\n7. ¿A qué hora empieza la película?\n8. No sé.\n","a1/verbs-present-regular.md":"---\nid: a1-verbs-present-regular\nlevel: A1\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: Present tense of regular -ar, -er, -ir verbs\nsummary: Drop -ar, -er or -ir and add one ending per person. One pattern gives you hundreds of verbs.\n---\n\n# Present tense of regular -ar, -er, -ir verbs\n\nEvery Spanish verb ends in **-ar**, **-er** or **-ir** in the dictionary: *hablar* (to speak), *comer* (to eat), *vivir* (to live). In the present tense you take off that ending and add a new one that shows **who** does the action. English adds only *-s* (I speak, she speaks); Spanish has six endings, one per person. The good news: once you know the pattern, it works for hundreds of regular verbs.\n\n## How it works\n\n| Person | hablar (to speak) | comer (to eat) | vivir (to live) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | habl**o** | com**o** | viv**o** |\n| tú | habl**as** | com**es** | viv**es** |\n| él, ella, usted | habl**a** | com**e** | viv**e** |\n| nosotros, nosotras | habl**amos** | com**emos** | viv**imos** |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | habl**áis** | com**éis** | viv**ís** |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | habl**an** | com**en** | viv**en** |\n\n- The **yo** form always ends in **-o**.\n- **-er** and **-ir** verbs share every ending except *nosotros* and *vosotros*: *comemos / vivimos*, *coméis / vivís*.\n- The pronoun is usually left out, because the ending shows the person: *Hablo inglés.* (I speak English.)\n- **Negative:** *no* before the verb: *No como carne.* (I don't eat meat.)\n- **Question:** the same verb with ¿ ?: *¿Hablas español?* (Do you speak Spanish?) Spanish has no \"do / does\".\n\n**Common regular verbs:** *-ar*: *hablar, trabajar* (to work), *estudiar* (to study), *escuchar* (to listen), *llegar* (to arrive), *comprar* (to buy), *tomar* (to take, to drink). *-er*: *comer, beber* (to drink), *leer* (to read), *aprender* (to learn), *comprender* (to understand). *-ir*: *vivir, escribir* (to write), *abrir* (to open), *recibir* (to receive).\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Things that are true now.** *Vivo en Bogotá.* (I live in Bogotá.)\n2. **Habits and routines.** *Trabajo de lunes a viernes.* (I work from Monday to Friday.)\n3. **Actions happening right now.** *¿Qué lees?* (What are you reading?) English uses \"-ing\" here; Spanish can use the simple present.\n4. **Near plans with a time word.** *Mañana trabajo hasta las seis.* (Tomorrow I'm working until six.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Hablo inglés y un poco de español. | I speak English and a little Spanish. |\n| ¿Dónde vives? | Where do you live? |\n| Mi hermano trabaja en un banco. | My brother works in a bank. |\n| No comemos carne. | We don't eat meat. |\n| Los niños aprenden muy rápido. | Children learn very fast. |\n| ¿Escribís en español? (Spain) | Do you (all) write in Spanish? |\n| Mis padres viven en el centro. | My parents live downtown. |\n| ¿Lees el periódico todos los días? | Do you read the newspaper every day? |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- Many verbs change their stem or have an irregular *yo* form (*quiero*, *tengo*, *hago*). They come in unit 4; see [[a1-verbs-present-irregular]].\n- *Tomar* is used for drinks and food in many places: *Tomo un café.* (I'm having a coffee.)\n- The *vosotros* forms carry a written accent: *habláis*, *coméis*, *vivís*.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Yo hablar español.* → ✓ *Hablo español.* — the verb needs a personal ending; *hablar* is only the dictionary form.\n- ✗ *Él comes pescado.* → ✓ *Él come pescado.* — *-es* is the *tú* ending; *él* takes *come*.\n- ✗ *Nosotros vivemos en Lima.* → ✓ *Nosotros vivimos en Lima.* — *-ir* verbs end in *-imos*.\n- ✗ *Él come no carne.* → ✓ *Él no come carne.* — *no* goes before the verb.\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete with the right form.\n\n1. Yo ___ (trabajar) en una tienda.\n2. ¿Tú ___ (comer) pescado?\n3. Ana ___ (vivir) en México.\n4. Nosotros ___ (aprender) español.\n5. Ellos no ___ (beber) café.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n6. Do you speak English? (to a friend)\n7. We live downtown.\n8. I don't read the newspaper.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. trabajo\n2. comes\n3. vive\n4. aprendemos\n5. beben\n6. ¿Hablas inglés?\n7. Vivimos en el centro.\n8. No leo el periódico.\n","a1/verbs-reflexive-routine.md":"---\nid: a1-verbs-reflexive-routine\nlevel: A1\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: Reflexive verbs for the daily routine\nsummary: Me levanto, me ducho, me acuesto. Reflexive verbs carry a small pronoun that changes with the person.\n---\n\n# Reflexive verbs for the daily routine\n\nMany everyday actions are **reflexive** in Spanish: you do them to yourself. English often says \"I get up\", \"I shower\", \"I go to bed\" with no extra word; Spanish adds a small pronoun: *me levanto*, *me ducho*, *me acuesto*. You already know one reflexive verb: *llamarse* (*me llamo Ana*). In the dictionary these verbs end in **-se**: *levantarse*, *ducharse*.\n\n## How it works\n\nThe pronoun (*me, te, se, nos, os, se*) goes **before** the conjugated verb.\n\n| Person | levantarse (to get up) | ducharse (to shower) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | **me** levanto | **me** ducho |\n| tú | **te** levantas | **te** duchas |\n| él, ella, usted | **se** levanta | **se** ducha |\n| nosotros, nosotras | **nos** levantamos | **nos** duchamos |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | **os** levantáis | **os** ducháis |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | **se** levantan | **se** duchan |\n\n- **Negative:** *no* before the pronoun: *No me levanto temprano.*\n- **Question:** *¿A qué hora te levantas?* (What time do you get up?)\n- Some are also stem-changing: *despertarse* (e → ie): *me despierto*; *acostarse* (o → ue): *me acuesto*; *vestirse* (e → i): *me visto*.\n\n**Routine verbs:** *despertarse* (to wake up), *levantarse* (to get up), *ducharse* (to shower), *lavarse* (to wash; *lavarse los dientes* = to brush your teeth, also *cepillarse los dientes*), *vestirse* (to get dressed), *peinarse* (to comb your hair), *acostarse* (to go to bed), *dormirse* (to fall asleep).\n\n**Not reflexive, but part of the day:** *desayunar* (to have breakfast), *comer* (to eat; in Spain and Mexico also \"to have lunch\"; many Latin American countries say *almorzar*), *cenar* (to have dinner), *trabajar*, *salir de casa*, *volver a casa*.\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Describing your day.** *Me levanto a las siete y me ducho.* (I get up at seven and shower.)\n2. **Asking about someone's routine.** *¿A qué hora te acuestas?* (What time do you go to bed?)\n3. **Saying what you do to your body.** *Me lavo las manos.* (I wash my hands.) Spanish uses *las*, not *mis*: the pronoun already shows whose hands.\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Me despierto a las seis y media. | I wake up at six thirty. |\n| ¿Te duchas por la mañana o por la noche? | Do you shower in the morning or at night? |\n| Mi hijo se viste solo. | My son gets dressed on his own. |\n| Nos acostamos tarde los sábados. | We go to bed late on Saturdays. |\n| Desayuno café con pan tostado. | I have coffee and toast for breakfast. |\n| Mis padres se levantan muy temprano. | My parents get up very early. |\n| No me peino nunca. | I never comb my hair. |\n| ¿A qué hora os levantáis? (Spain) | What time do you (all) get up? |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- With an infinitive, the pronoun can go at the end and joins the verb: *Quiero ducharme.* = *Me quiero duchar.* (I want to take a shower.)\n- Lunch is the main meal in Spain and much of Latin America; in Spain it is often around 2 p.m.\n- More on times in [[a1-functions-time-dates]].\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Levanto a las siete.* → ✓ *Me levanto a las siete.* — \"get up\" needs the reflexive pronoun.\n- ✗ *Me lavo mis dientes.* → ✓ *Me lavo los dientes.* — with body parts Spanish uses *los / las*, not *mis*.\n- ✗ *Ella me ducha.* (meaning \"she showers\") → ✓ *Ella se ducha.* — the pronoun matches the person: *se* for *ella*.\n- ✗ *Me acosto a las once.* → ✓ *Me acuesto a las once.* — *acostarse* changes o to ue.\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete with the pronoun and the verb.\n\n1. Yo ___ ___ (levantarse) a las siete.\n2. ¿Tú ___ ___ (ducharse) por la mañana?\n3. Ana ___ ___ (acostarse) tarde.\n4. Nosotros ___ ___ (despertarse) temprano.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. I get dressed.\n6. What time do you go to bed? (to a friend)\n7. My parents get up at six.\n8. I brush my teeth.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. me levanto\n2. te duchas\n3. se acuesta\n4. nos despertamos\n5. Me visto.\n6. ¿A qué hora te acuestas?\n7. Mis padres se levantan a las seis.\n8. Me lavo los dientes.\n","a1/verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics.md":"---\nid: a1-verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics\nlevel: A1\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: ser vs estar: the basics\nsummary: Ser for what something is; estar for where it is and how it is now. Some adjectives change meaning with the verb.\n---\n\n# ser vs estar: the basics\n\nYou now know both verbs for \"to be\". This page puts them side by side, because choosing between them is one of the first real challenges in Spanish. The short version: **ser** says what someone or something **is** (identity, origin, job, character), and **estar** says **where** it is and **how** it is at the moment. The same adjective can go with both verbs and change the meaning: *Es aburrido* (he is boring), *Está aburrido* (he is bored).\n\n## How it works\n\n| ser | estar |\n| --- | --- |\n| identity, name: *Es Ana.* | location: *Ana está en Madrid.* |\n| origin: *Es de Chile.* | feelings now: *Está contenta.* |\n| job: *Es médica.* | states now: *Está enferma.* |\n| character, looks: *Es simpática. Es alta.* | how it looks or tastes now: *La sopa está rica.* |\n| time and dates: *Son las tres. Hoy es lunes.* | results of a change: *La puerta está abierta.* |\n| where an event takes place: *La fiesta es en mi casa.* | *bien* / *mal*: *Estoy bien.* |\n\n**Some adjectives change meaning:**\n\n| with ser | with estar |\n| --- | --- |\n| *Es aburrido.* He is boring. | *Está aburrido.* He is bored. |\n| *Es listo.* He is clever. | *Está listo.* He is ready. |\n| *Es rico.* He is rich. | *Está rico.* (food) It tastes good. |\n| *Es malo.* He is bad. | *Está malo.* He is sick (mainly Spain; everywhere: *está enfermo*). / (food) It tastes bad. |\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Ask \"what is it like in general?\"** → *ser*. *Mi ciudad es tranquila.* (My city is quiet.)\n2. **Ask \"how is it now?\" or \"where is it?\"** → *estar*. *Hoy la ciudad está tranquila.* (The city is quiet today.) *La ciudad está en el norte.*\n3. **Describing a person:** *ser* for who they are, *estar* for how they feel. *Pedro es tímido, pero hoy está contento.* (Pedro is shy, but today he's happy.)\n4. **Talking about food:** *ser* for what it is (*La paella es un plato español*), *estar* for how it tastes now (*La paella está muy rica*).\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Soy de Colombia, pero estoy en Madrid. | I'm from Colombia, but I'm in Madrid. |\n| Laura es médica. Hoy está cansada. | Laura is a doctor. Today she's tired. |\n| La casa es grande y está en el centro. | The house is big and it's downtown. |\n| Mi hermano es muy alegre. | My brother is a very cheerful person. |\n| ¿Estás bien? —No, estoy un poco mal. | Are you OK? —No, I'm a bit unwell. |\n| La película es aburrida. | The movie is boring. |\n| Los niños están aburridos. | The children are bored. |\n| ¿Estás listo? Son las ocho. | Are you ready? It's eight o'clock. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *Estar* + *muerto* (dead) is the normal form, although death is permanent: *El pez está muerto.*\n- Some words take only one verb: *ser* + nouns (*Es profesor*), *estar* + *de acuerdo* (*Estoy de acuerdo*, I agree).\n- A quick check: if you can add \"right now\" without changing the sense, *estar* is usually the answer.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Soy cansado.* → ✓ *Estoy cansado.* — feeling tired is a state now: *estar*.\n- ✗ *Está médico.* → ✓ *Es médico.* — a job is what someone is: *ser*.\n- ✗ *Madrid es en España.* → ✓ *Madrid está en España.* — location of places takes *estar*.\n- ✗ *La fiesta está en mi casa.* → ✓ *La fiesta es en mi casa.* — where an event takes place uses *ser*.\n- ✗ *Estoy aburrido* (meaning \"I'm a boring person\") → ✓ *Soy aburrido.* — with *estar* it means \"I'm bored\".\n\n## Practice\n\n*Ser* or *estar*? Write the right form.\n\n1. Mi madre ___ profesora.\n2. Hoy (yo) ___ muy cansado.\n3. Lima ___ en Perú.\n4. La fiesta ___ en el bar de Juan.\n5. Carlos ___ de Argentina.\n6. ¿Dónde ___ los niños?\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n7. The children are bored.\n8. Are you ready? (to a friend; you are talking to a woman)\n\n## Answers\n\n1. es\n2. estoy\n3. está\n4. es\n5. es\n6. están\n7. Los niños están aburridos.\n8. ¿Estás lista?\n","a1/verbs-ser.md":"---\nid: a1-verbs-ser\nlevel: A1\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: ser: identity, origin and profession\nsummary: The first \"to be\": say who you are, where you are from, what you do and what someone is like.\n---\n\n# ser: identity, origin and profession\n\nEnglish has one verb \"to be\". Spanish has two, *ser* and *estar*, and they are not interchangeable. This page is about *ser*, the verb for what something **is**: a name, a nationality, a job, a lasting quality. You will meet *estar* (where something is, how someone feels) in unit 3. *Ser* is irregular, so learn its six forms by heart.\n\n## How it works\n\n| Person | ser | English |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | **soy** | I am |\n| tú | **eres** | you are |\n| él, ella, usted | **es** | he / she is, you are (formal) |\n| nosotros, nosotras | **somos** | we are |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | **sois** | you are (plural) |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | **son** | they are, you are (plural) |\n\n- **Negative:** put *no* before the verb: *No soy de aquí.* (I'm not from here.)\n- **Question:** the same words, with ¿ ? and rising intonation: *¿Eres de México?* (Are you from Mexico?)\n- **Origin:** *ser* + *de* + place: *Soy de Chile.*\n- **Jobs:** no *un* / *una* before the job: *Es médico.* (He is a doctor.)\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Name and identity.** *Soy Marta.* (I'm Marta.) *Es mi amigo.* (He is my friend.)\n2. **Origin and nationality.** *Somos de Colombia.* (We're from Colombia.) *Es español.* (He is Spanish.) Nationalities are written with a small letter.\n3. **Job.** *Soy profesor.* (I'm a teacher; a woman says *profesora*.) *Son estudiantes.* (They are students.)\n4. **What someone or something is like** (a lasting quality). *Es inteligente.* (She is smart.) *Eres muy amable.* (You are very kind.)\n5. **Day and date.** *Hoy es lunes.* (Today is Monday.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Soy Daniel. Soy de Canadá. | I'm Daniel. I'm from Canada. |\n| ¿Eres estudiante? | Are you a student? |\n| Mi madre es médica. | My mother is a doctor. |\n| No somos de aquí. | We're not from here. |\n| ¿De dónde es usted? | Where are you from? (formal) |\n| Estos son Ana y Luis. Son de Madrid. | This is Ana and Luis. They're from Madrid. |\n| Mi profesora es muy simpática. | My teacher is very nice. |\n| ¿Sois hermanos? (Spain) | Are you brothers? / Are you brother and sister? |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *Es* can mean \"he is\", \"she is\", \"it is\" and \"you are\" (formal). The context tells you which; add *él*, *ella* or *usted* only if it is not clear.\n- With a job plus a description, the article comes back: *Es un médico muy bueno.* (He is a very good doctor.)\n- For age Spanish uses *tener*, not *ser*: *Tengo 25 años.* See [[a1-verbs-tener]].\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Soy un profesor.* → ✓ *Soy profesor.* — no *un* / *una* before a job on its own.\n- ✗ *Soy 20 años.* → ✓ *Tengo 20 años.* — age is \"having years\" in Spanish.\n- ✗ *Yo es de Chile.* → ✓ *Yo soy de Chile.* — each person has its own form: *yo soy*, *él es*.\n- ✗ *Es en Madrid.* (where a person is) → ✓ *Está en Madrid.* — location of people and things takes *estar*; *ser de* is for origin (events are different; you will see them later).\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete with the right form of *ser*.\n\n1. Yo ___ de Perú.\n2. ¿Tú ___ profesora?\n3. Nosotros ___ amigos.\n4. Ellas ___ de Italia.\n5. Usted ___ muy amable.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n6. I'm not from here.\n7. She is a nurse.\n8. Where are you from? (to a friend)\n\n## Answers\n\n1. soy\n2. eres\n3. somos\n4. son\n5. es\n6. No soy de aquí.\n7. Es enfermera. / Ella es enfermera.\n8. ¿De dónde eres? / ¿De dónde eres tú?\n","a1/verbs-tener.md":"---\nid: a1-verbs-tener\nlevel: A1\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: tener: possession, age and common expressions\nsummary: Tener means \"to have\". Spanish also uses it for age and for hunger, thirst, heat, cold and fear.\n---\n\n# tener: possession, age and common expressions\n\n*Tener* means \"to have\": *Tengo un perro.* (I have a dog.) Spanish uses it in more places than English does. Your age is something you \"have\": *Tengo 30 años.* And many feelings of the body are things you \"have\" too: *Tengo hambre* (I'm hungry, word for word \"I have hunger\"). Where English says \"I am…\", Spanish often says *tengo*.\n\n## How it works\n\n| Person | tener | English |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | **tengo** | I have |\n| tú | **tienes** | you have |\n| él, ella, usted | **tiene** | he / she has, you have (formal) |\n| nosotros, nosotras | **tenemos** | we have |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | **tenéis** | you have (plural) |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | **tienen** | they have, you have (plural) |\n\n- The *yo* form ends in *-go*: *tengo*. The stem changes to *tie-* in *tienes*, *tiene* and *tienen*, but not in *tengo*, *tenemos* and *tenéis*.\n- **Negative:** *No tengo hermanos.* After *no*, a single countable thing often has no article: *No tengo coche.* (more natural than *No tengo un coche*)\n- **Question:** *¿Tienes hermanos?* (Do you have any brothers or sisters?)\n\n**Expressions with tener** (the noun after *tener* does not change):\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| tener hambre | to be hungry |\n| tener sed | to be thirsty |\n| tener calor / frío | to be hot / cold (a person) |\n| tener sueño | to be sleepy |\n| tener miedo | to be afraid |\n| tener prisa | to be in a hurry |\n| tener razón | to be right |\n\nTo say \"very\", use *mucho* or *mucha* with the gender of the noun: *mucho frío*, *mucho calor*, but *mucha hambre*, *mucha sed*, *mucha prisa*.\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Possession.** *Tengo un coche nuevo.* (I have a new car.)\n2. **Family and friends.** *Tenemos dos hijos.* (We have two children.) *¿Tienes novio?* (Do you have a boyfriend?)\n3. **Age.** *¿Cuántos años tienes?* (How old are you?) *Tengo veintiún años.* (I'm twenty-one.)\n4. **How your body feels.** *Tengo frío.* (I'm cold.) *¿Tienes hambre?* (Are you hungry?)\n5. **Agreeing with someone.** *Tienes razón.* (You're right.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Tengo dos hermanos y una hermana. | I have two brothers and a sister. |\n| ¿Cuántos años tiene usted? | How old are you? (formal) |\n| Mi abuela tiene ochenta años. | My grandmother is eighty. |\n| No tenemos coche. | We don't have a car. |\n| ¿Tenéis hambre? (Spain) | Are you (all) hungry? |\n| Los niños tienen sueño. | The children are sleepy. |\n| Tengo mucho calor. | I'm very hot. |\n| Tienes razón. | You're right. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *Tengo calor* is about how **you** feel. For the weather, Spanish says *Hace calor* (it's hot); you will learn it later.\n- *Estar caliente* about a person can have a sexual meaning. Say *tengo calor*.\n- *Tener que* + infinitive means \"to have to\": *Tengo que trabajar.* It comes in A2.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Soy 25 años.* → ✓ *Tengo 25 años.* — age takes *tener*, not *ser*.\n- ✗ *Estoy caliente.* (I feel hot) → ✓ *Tengo calor.* — the body feeling is *tener calor*.\n- ✗ *Tengo mucho hambre.* → ✓ *Tengo mucha hambre.* — *hambre* is feminine (*el hambre*, like *el agua*).\n- ✗ *Estoy hambre.* → ✓ *Tengo hambre.* — *hambre* is a noun (hunger), so you \"have\" it.\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete with the right form of *tener*.\n\n1. Yo ___ un gato.\n2. ¿Cuántos años ___ (tú)?\n3. Nosotros ___ dos hijas.\n4. Ellos no ___ coche.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. I'm hungry.\n6. She is twenty years old.\n7. Are you cold? (to a friend)\n8. You're right. (formal)\n\n## Answers\n\n1. tengo\n2. tienes\n3. tenemos\n4. tienen\n5. Tengo hambre.\n6. Tiene veinte años. / Ella tiene veinte años.\n7. ¿Tienes frío?\n8. Usted tiene razón. / Tiene razón.\n","a1/vocabulary-family-people.md":"---\nid: a1-vocabulary-family-people\nlevel: A1\ncategory: vocabulary\ntitle: Family, describing people, nationalities, jobs\nsummary: Words for your family, how people look and what they are like, where they are from and what they do.\n---\n\n# Family, describing people, nationalities, jobs\n\nWith *ser* and *tener* you can now talk about the people in your life: *Tengo una hermana. Es alta y muy simpática. Es médica.* Remember three rules from earlier pages: adjectives and many nouns change for women (*el médico*, *la médica*), nationalities take a small letter, and there is no *un* / *una* before a job on its own.\n\n## Words\n\n**Family**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el padre / la madre | father / mother |\n| los padres | parents (or fathers) |\n| el hermano / la hermana | brother / sister |\n| los hermanos | brothers, or brothers and sisters |\n| el hijo / la hija | son / daughter |\n| los hijos | children (sons and daughters) |\n| el abuelo / la abuela | grandfather / grandmother |\n| el tío / la tía | uncle / aunt |\n| el primo / la prima | cousin (male / female) |\n| el marido / la mujer | husband / wife |\n| el novio / la novia | boyfriend / girlfriend |\n\n**Describing people**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| alto / bajo | tall / short |\n| joven / mayor | young / older, elderly |\n| rubio / moreno | blond / dark-haired |\n| delgado | slim |\n| simpático / antipático | nice / unfriendly |\n| inteligente | smart |\n| divertido | fun |\n| tímido | shy |\n\n**Nationalities** (small letter)\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| español / española | Spanish |\n| mexicano / mexicana | Mexican |\n| colombiano / colombiana | Colombian |\n| argentino / argentina | Argentinian |\n| estadounidense | American (from the US) |\n| canadiense | Canadian |\n| inglés / inglesa | English |\n| alemán / alemana | German |\n\n**Jobs**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el médico / la médica | doctor |\n| el enfermero / la enfermera | nurse |\n| el profesor / la profesora | teacher |\n| el / la estudiante | student |\n| el camarero / la camarera | waiter / waitress (Spain; *mesero* in Mexico, *mozo* in Argentina) |\n| el ingeniero / la ingeniera | engineer |\n| el / la policía | police officer (*la policía* also means \"the police\") |\n| el abogado / la abogada | lawyer |\n\n## Word partners\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Tengo dos hermanos. | I have two brothers (or a brother and a sister). |\n| Es hijo único. | He's an only child. |\n| Mi hermana es alta y morena. | My sister is tall and dark-haired. |\n| Mis padres son muy simpáticos. | My parents are very nice. |\n| Mi abuelo es mayor y tiene noventa años. | My grandfather is old; he's ninety. |\n| Es de Colombia, es colombiana. | She's from Colombia; she's Colombian. |\n| Mi madre es profesora. | My mother is a teacher. |\n| ¿Tienes novio? | Do you have a boyfriend? |\n| Es muy divertido. | He's a lot of fun. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *El marido* / *la mujer* are used everywhere; *el esposo* / *la esposa* are also common, especially in Latin America.\n- *Mayor* is the polite word for an old person. *Viejo* can sound rude about people.\n- *Americano* can mean anyone from the Americas; *estadounidense* is only \"from the US\".\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *mis parientes* (meaning \"my parents\") → ✓ *mis padres* — *parientes* means \"relatives\".\n- ✗ *Es Mexicana.* → ✓ *Es mexicana.* — nationalities take a small letter.\n- ✗ *Mi hermana es alto.* → ✓ *Mi hermana es alta.* — the adjective agrees with *hermana*.\n- ✗ *Ella es inglés.* → ✓ *Ella es inglesa.* — the feminine adds *-a*.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the female form.\n\n1. el abuelo\n2. el profesor\n3. Es español.\n4. Es alemán.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. my parents\n6. I have a sister.\n7. He's tall and nice.\n8. She's a nurse.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. la abuela\n2. la profesora\n3. Es española.\n4. Es alemana.\n5. mis padres\n6. Tengo una hermana.\n7. Es alto y simpático.\n8. Es enfermera.\n","a1/vocabulary-food-shopping.md":"---\nid: a1-vocabulary-food-shopping\nlevel: A1\ncategory: vocabulary\ntitle: Food, drink, shops and prices\nsummary: Everyday food and drink, the shops that sell them, quantities and how to ask what something costs.\n---\n\n# Food, drink, shops and prices\n\nFood is the easiest place to practice: you buy it every day. Learn each noun with its article, and notice two habits. Spanish talks about food in general with the article (*Me gusta el pan*), but asks for an amount without it (*Quiero pan*). And prices use *costar* (o → ue): *¿Cuánto cuesta?* for one thing, *¿Cuánto cuestan?* for several.\n\n## Words\n\n**Food**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el pan | bread |\n| el arroz | rice |\n| la pasta | pasta |\n| la carne | meat |\n| el pollo | chicken |\n| el pescado | fish (as food) |\n| el huevo | egg |\n| el queso | cheese |\n| el jamón | ham |\n| la fruta | fruit |\n| la manzana | apple |\n| el plátano (Spain, Mexico) / la banana, el banano (other parts of Latin America) | banana |\n| la naranja | orange |\n| el tomate | tomato |\n| la patata (Spain) / la papa (Latin America) | potato |\n| la verdura / las verduras | vegetables |\n| la ensalada | salad |\n\n**Drinks**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el agua (f.) | water |\n| el café / el café con leche | coffee / coffee with milk |\n| el té | tea |\n| la leche | milk |\n| el zumo (Spain) / el jugo (Latin America) | juice |\n| el vino / la cerveza | wine / beer |\n\n**Shops and amounts**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el supermercado | supermarket |\n| el mercado | market |\n| la panadería | bakery |\n| la frutería | fruit store |\n| un kilo de | a kilo of |\n| medio kilo de | half a kilo of |\n| una botella de | a bottle of |\n| un paquete de | a package of |\n| una barra de pan | a loaf of bread (a baguette) |\n| el euro / el peso / el dólar | euro / peso / dollar |\n\n## Word partners\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Cuánto cuesta el queso? | How much is the cheese? |\n| ¿Cuánto cuestan las manzanas? | How much are the apples? |\n| Cuesta tres euros con cincuenta. | It costs three euros fifty. |\n| Un kilo de tomates, por favor. | A kilo of tomatoes, please. |\n| Quiero una botella de agua. | I'd like a bottle of water. |\n| No como carne. | I don't eat meat. |\n| Tomo el café con leche y sin azúcar. | I take my coffee with milk and no sugar. |\n| ¿Algo más? —No, nada más, gracias. | Anything else? —No, nothing else, thanks. |\n| Me gusta mucho la fruta. | I really like fruit. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *El pescado* is fish as food; a live fish is *el pez*.\n- Many prices are said with *con*: *2,50 €* is *dos euros con cincuenta* (or *dos cincuenta*). Spain and much of Latin America write a decimal comma: *2,50*; Mexico and some other countries use a decimal point: *2.50*.\n- *Papa* (potato) is Latin American; in Spain it is *patata*. *Jugo* is the word for juice in Latin America; *zumo* is the word in Spain.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *¿Cuánto cuesta los tomates?* → ✓ *¿Cuánto cuestan los tomates?* — the verb agrees with the plural noun.\n- ✗ *Me gusta café.* → ✓ *Me gusta el café.* — food in general takes the article after *gustar*.\n- ✗ *un kilo tomates* → ✓ *un kilo de tomates* — amounts need *de*.\n- ✗ *la agua* → ✓ *el agua* — *agua* is feminine but takes *el* in the singular.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the word with the article.\n\n1. bread\n2. cheese\n3. water\n4. chicken\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. How much are the oranges?\n6. A kilo of apples, please.\n7. I don't eat fish.\n8. A bottle of milk.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. el pan\n2. el queso\n3. el agua\n4. el pollo\n5. ¿Cuánto cuestan las naranjas?\n6. Un kilo de manzanas, por favor.\n7. No como pescado.\n8. Una botella de leche.\n","a1/vocabulary-home-city.md":"---\nid: a1-vocabulary-home-city\nlevel: A1\ncategory: vocabulary\ntitle: Home, furniture and places in town\nsummary: Rooms, furniture and the places you need in a town, with hay and estar to say what there is and where it is.\n---\n\n# Home, furniture and places in town\n\nWith *hay* and *estar* you can now describe where you live: *Mi casa tiene dos dormitorios. Hay un supermercado en la esquina. La farmacia está al lado del banco.* Learn every noun with its article. Words for \"apartment\" differ by country, so both are given; you will be understood with either.\n\n## Words\n\n**The home**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| la casa | the house, the home |\n| el piso (Spain) / el departamento (Mexico, Argentina, Chile) / el apartamento (understood everywhere) | the apartment |\n| la habitación / el cuarto | the room, the bedroom |\n| el dormitorio | the bedroom |\n| el salón (Spain) / la sala (Latin America) | the living room |\n| la cocina | the kitchen |\n| el baño | the bathroom |\n| el jardín | the garden, the yard |\n| la ventana | the window |\n| la puerta | the door |\n\n**Furniture**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| la cama | the bed |\n| la mesa | the table |\n| la silla | the chair |\n| el sofá | the sofa |\n| el armario | the closet, the wardrobe |\n| la estantería | the bookcase, the shelves |\n| la lámpara | the lamp |\n| la nevera (Spain) / el refrigerador (Latin America) | the fridge |\n\n**Places in town**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| la calle | the street |\n| la plaza | the square |\n| el banco | the bank |\n| la farmacia | the pharmacy |\n| el supermercado | the supermarket |\n| la tienda | the store |\n| el restaurante | the restaurant |\n| el hospital | the hospital |\n| la estación (de tren) | the (train) station |\n| la parada de autobús | the bus stop |\n| el parque | the park |\n| el museo | the museum |\n| la biblioteca | the library |\n\n**Where?**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| cerca (de) / lejos (de) | near / far (from) |\n| al lado de | next to |\n| enfrente de | opposite |\n| entre | between |\n| en la esquina | on the corner |\n\n## Word partners\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Vivo en un piso pequeño. (Spain) | I live in a small apartment. |\n| La cocina es muy grande. | The kitchen is very big. |\n| En mi habitación hay una cama y una mesa. | In my room there's a bed and a table. |\n| ¿Hay un banco por aquí? | Is there a bank around here? |\n| La farmacia está al lado del banco. | The pharmacy is next to the bank. |\n| El museo está enfrente del parque. | The museum is opposite the park. |\n| La estación está lejos del centro. | The station is far from downtown. |\n| La parada de autobús está en la esquina. | The bus stop is on the corner. |\n| Mi casa está cerca de la playa. | My house is near the beach. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *De* + *el* always becomes *del*: *al lado del banco*, *cerca del parque* (but *cerca de la plaza*).\n- *Librería* is a bookstore; a library is *la biblioteca*.\n- *El baño* is the bathroom at home; in a restaurant people often ask *¿Dónde está el baño?* or, in Spain, *¿Dónde están los servicios?*\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Hay la farmacia en la plaza.* → ✓ *La farmacia está en la plaza.* — a known place (*la*) takes *está*.\n- ✗ *la librería* (meaning \"the library\") → ✓ *la biblioteca* — *librería* means \"bookstore\".\n- ✗ *al lado de el banco* → ✓ *al lado del banco* — *de* + *el* becomes *del*.\n- ✗ *El hospital está cerca la estación.* → ✓ *El hospital está cerca de la estación.* — *cerca* needs *de* before a place.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the word with *el* or *la*.\n\n1. kitchen\n2. bed\n3. pharmacy\n4. library\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. Is there a supermarket near here?\n6. The bank is next to the park.\n7. My apartment has two bedrooms. (Spain)\n8. The station is far from downtown.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. la cocina\n2. la cama\n3. la farmacia\n4. la biblioteca\n5. ¿Hay un supermercado cerca? / ¿Hay un supermercado cerca de aquí? / ¿Hay un supermercado por aquí?\n6. El banco está al lado del parque.\n7. Mi piso tiene dos dormitorios. / Mi piso tiene dos habitaciones.\n8. La estación está lejos del centro.\n","a1/vocabulary-numbers-days-months.md":"---\nid: a1-vocabulary-numbers-days-months\nlevel: A1\ncategory: vocabulary\ntitle: Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months\nsummary: Count to one hundred, name the days and months, and say \"on Monday\" and \"in July\" the Spanish way.\n---\n\n# Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months\n\nNumbers, days and months are everywhere: prices, phone numbers, plans. Two things surprise English speakers. Days and months are written with a **small letter** in Spanish. And you say \"on Monday\" without any word for \"on\": just *el lunes*.\n\n## Words\n\n**Numbers 0–20**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| cero, uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco | 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 |\n| seis, siete, ocho, nueve, diez | 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 |\n| once, doce, trece, catorce, quince | 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 |\n| dieciséis, diecisiete, dieciocho, diecinueve, veinte | 16, 17, 18, 19, 20 |\n\n**Numbers 21–100**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| veintiuno, veintidós, veintitrés | 21, 22, 23 |\n| veinticuatro … veintinueve | 24 … 29 |\n| treinta, treinta y uno, treinta y dos | 30, 31, 32 |\n| cuarenta, cincuenta, sesenta | 40, 50, 60 |\n| setenta, ochenta, noventa | 70, 80, 90 |\n| noventa y nueve | 99 |\n| cien | 100 |\n\n- 16–29 are **one word**: *diecisiete*, *veintiocho*. From 31 they are **three words** with *y*: *cuarenta y cinco*.\n- Watch the accents: *dieciséis*, *veintidós*, *veintitrés*, *veintiséis*.\n- *Uno* becomes *un* before a masculine noun and *una* before a feminine one: *un euro*, *una persona*, *veintiún años*.\n\n**Days of the week** (the week starts on Monday)\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el lunes | Monday |\n| el martes | Tuesday |\n| el miércoles | Wednesday |\n| el jueves | Thursday |\n| el viernes | Friday |\n| el sábado | Saturday |\n| el domingo | Sunday |\n| el fin de semana | the weekend |\n\n**Months**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| enero, febrero, marzo | January, February, March |\n| abril, mayo, junio | April, May, June |\n| julio, agosto, septiembre | July, August, September |\n| octubre, noviembre, diciembre | October, November, December |\n\n## Word partners\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Hoy es martes. | Today is Tuesday. |\n| el lunes | on Monday (this coming Monday) |\n| los lunes | on Mondays (every Monday) |\n| de lunes a viernes | from Monday to Friday |\n| el fin de semana | on the weekend |\n| en julio | in July |\n| en verano | in summer |\n| el número de teléfono | the phone number |\n| Mi número es seis, cinco, cero… | My number is six, five, zero… |\n| ¿Cuánto es? —Son veinte euros. | How much is it? —It's twenty euros. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- Phone numbers are read digit by digit or in pairs: 61 20 34 → *sesenta y uno, veinte, treinta y cuatro*.\n- *Septiembre* is also spelled *setiembre* in some countries.\n- Days ending in *-s* do not change in the plural: *el lunes, los lunes*. *Sábado* and *domingo* add *-s*: *los sábados*.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Hoy es Lunes.* → ✓ *Hoy es lunes.* — days and months take a small letter.\n- ✗ *en el lunes* → ✓ *el lunes* — Spanish has no word for \"on\" with days.\n- ✗ *veinte y dos* → ✓ *veintidós* — today 21–29 are written as one word.\n- ✗ *en Julio* → ✓ *en julio* — months also take a small letter.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the number in words.\n\n1. 15\n2. 22\n3. 48\n4. 100\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. on Saturday\n6. on Fridays\n7. in October\n8. Today is Wednesday.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. quince\n2. veintidós\n3. cuarenta y ocho\n4. cien\n5. el sábado\n6. los viernes\n7. en octubre\n8. Hoy es miércoles.\n","a2/functions-describing.md":"---\nid: a2-functions-describing\nlevel: A2\ncategory: functions\ntitle: Describe people, places and things\nsummary: Say what someone looks like and is like, describe a home or a town, and explain what an object is, now and in the past.\n---\n\n# Describe people, places and things\n\nSpanish describes with four verbs where English mostly uses \"to be\" and \"to have\": *ser* for what someone or something is like, *estar* for where it is and how it is now, *tener* for features, and *llevar* for what someone wears. *Es un piso pequeño pero muy luminoso.* (Spain: It's a small but very bright apartment.) To describe the past, use the imperfect: *La casa era vieja y tenía un jardín.*\n\n## Key phrases\n\n**Asking**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Cómo es tu nuevo jefe? | What's your new boss like? |\n| ¿Cómo era tu barrio? | What was your neighborhood like? |\n| ¿Cómo es tu piso? (Spain) / ¿Cómo es tu departamento? (Latin America) | What's your apartment like? |\n| ¿De qué color es? | What color is it? |\n| ¿De qué es? | What is it made of? |\n\n**People**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Es alto y delgado. / Es bajita. | He's tall and slim. / She's a bit short. |\n| Tiene el pelo largo y rizado. | She has long, curly hair. |\n| Tiene los ojos verdes. | He has green eyes. |\n| Lleva gafas (Spain). / Usa lentes (Latin America). | He wears glasses. |\n| Es muy simpática y un poco tímida. | She's very nice and a bit shy. |\n| Parece muy tranquilo. | He seems very calm. |\n\n**Places**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Es pequeño pero muy luminoso. | It's small but very bright. |\n| Está en el centro, cerca del metro. | It's downtown, near the subway. |\n| Tiene dos dormitorios y una terraza. | It has two bedrooms and a terrace. |\n| Es un barrio tranquilo / ruidoso. | It's a quiet / noisy neighborhood. |\n\n**Things**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Es de madera / de metal / de plástico. | It's made of wood / metal / plastic. |\n| Es redondo / cuadrado. | It's round / square. |\n| Sirve para abrir botellas. | It's used for opening bottles. |\n\n## Dialogue\n\nTwo friends in Mexico City talk about a new apartment.\n\n> **Sofía:** ¿Qué tal el departamento nuevo? ¿Cómo es? (How's the new apartment? What's it like?)\n> **Diego:** Es pequeño, pero muy luminoso. Tiene una terraza con plantas. (It's small, but very bright. It has a terrace with plants.)\n> **Sofía:** ¡Qué bonito! ¿Y dónde está? (How nice! And where is it?)\n> **Diego:** Está en Coyoacán, cerca del metro. Es un barrio muy tranquilo. (It's in Coyoacán, near the subway. It's a very quiet neighborhood.)\n> **Sofía:** ¿Y los vecinos? (And the neighbors?)\n> **Diego:** La vecina de al lado es una señora mayor muy simpática. Tiene el pelo blanco y siempre lleva un sombrero rojo. (The neighbor next door is a very nice older lady. She has white hair and always wears a red hat.)\n> **Sofía:** ¿Y tu departamento anterior? (And your old apartment?)\n> **Diego:** Era grande, pero muy ruidoso y oscuro. No me gustaba nada. (It was big, but very noisy and dark. I didn't like it at all.)\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *Ser* or *estar* with the same adjective: *Es guapo* (he is good-looking) / *Está guapo hoy* (he looks good today). *Es aburrido* (he is boring) / *Está aburrido* (he is bored).\n- *Bajito*, *gordito*: the ending *-ito* makes a description softer and friendlier.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Es pelo largo.* → ✓ *Tiene el pelo largo.* — features take *tener*.\n- ✗ *Es una persona muy simpático.* → ✓ *Es una persona muy simpática.* — *persona* is feminine, for men too.\n- ✗ *Está una chica muy simpática.* → ✓ *Es una chica muy simpática.* — *ser* + a noun describes someone.\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete with *es*, *está*, *tiene* or *lleva*.\n\n1. Mi hermano ___ alto y delgado.\n2. Mi abuela ___ el pelo blanco.\n3. El hotel ___ cerca de la playa.\n4. Hoy Laura ___ un vestido azul.\n\nWrite the question.\n\n5. Ask what your friend's new boss is like.\n6. Ask what the table is made of.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n7. My apartment is small but very bright. (Spain)\n8. When I was a child, my neighborhood was very quiet.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. es\n2. tiene\n3. está\n4. lleva\n5. ¿Cómo es tu nuevo jefe? / ¿Cómo es tu jefe nuevo? (jefa: nueva)\n6. ¿De qué es la mesa?\n7. Mi piso es pequeño pero muy luminoso.\n8. Cuando era niño, mi barrio era muy tranquilo. / Cuando era niña, mi barrio era muy tranquilo.\n","a2/functions-weekend-vacation.md":"---\nid: a2-functions-weekend-vacation\nlevel: A2\ncategory: functions\ntitle: Say what you did last weekend or on vacation\nsummary: Ask about someone's weekend or trip, tell what you did in order, say how it was and react.\n---\n\n# Say what you did last weekend or on vacation\n\n\"How was your weekend?\" is a typical Monday question, and now you have the tense to answer it: *El sábado fui a la playa y comí paella.* Tell the actions in order with *primero*, *después* and *al final*. \"I had a good time\" is *Lo pasé bien.*\n\n## Key phrases\n\n**Asking**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Qué tal el fin de semana? | How was your weekend? |\n| ¿Qué hiciste el sábado? | What did you do on Saturday? |\n| ¿Adónde fuiste de vacaciones? | Where did you go on vacation? |\n| ¿Con quién fuiste? | Who did you go with? |\n| ¿Cómo fue el viaje? | How was the trip? |\n| ¿Cuánto tiempo estuviste allí? | How long were you there? |\n\n**Answering**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| El sábado fui a la playa. | On Saturday I went to the beach. |\n| Me quedé en casa y descansé. | I stayed at home and rested. |\n| Salí con unos amigos. | I went out with some friends. |\n| Visité a mis abuelos. | I visited my grandparents. |\n| Fuimos de excursión a la montaña. | We went on a trip to the mountains. |\n| Lo pasé muy bien. (Latin America also: La pasé muy bien.) | I had a great time. |\n| Fue genial. / Fue un desastre. | It was great. / It was a disaster. |\n| Me gustó mucho. | I liked it a lot. |\n\n**Telling it in order**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Primero… después… luego… al final… | First… then… after that… in the end… |\n| Por la mañana… por la tarde… por la noche… (Latin America also: en la…) | In the morning… in the afternoon… at night… |\n\n**Reacting**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¡Qué bien! / ¡Qué suerte! | How nice! / Lucky you! |\n| ¡Qué pena! | What a shame! |\n\n## Dialogue\n\nMonday morning at work in Madrid.\n\n> **Elena:** ¡Hola, Tom! ¿Qué tal el fin de semana? (Hi, Tom! How was your weekend?)\n> **Tom:** Muy bien, gracias. El sábado fui a Segovia con mi novia. (Really good, thanks. On Saturday I went to Segovia with my girlfriend.)\n> **Elena:** ¡Qué bien! ¿Y qué hicisteis? (How nice! And what did you two do?)\n> **Tom:** Primero visitamos el acueducto y luego comimos en un restaurante del centro. (First we visited the aqueduct and then we had lunch at a restaurant downtown.)\n> **Elena:** ¿Y qué tal? (And how was it?)\n> **Tom:** Lo pasamos muy bien. ¿Y tú? ¿Qué hiciste? (We had a great time. And you? What did you do?)\n> **Elena:** Nada especial. Me quedé en casa porque llovió todo el fin de semana. (Nothing special. I stayed at home because it rained all weekend.)\n> **Tom:** ¡Qué pena! (What a shame!)\n\n## Culture note\n\n- In Spain Elena says *hicisteis* (vosotros); in Latin America, *¿Y qué hicieron?*\n- *Un puente* is a long weekend: when a holiday falls on a Tuesday or a Thursday, many people in Spain take the Monday or Friday off too.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Tuve un buen tiempo.* → ✓ *Lo pasé bien.* — \"have a good time\" is not translated word for word.\n- ✗ *Fui en la playa.* → ✓ *Fui a la playa.* — after *ir*, the place takes *a*.\n- ✗ *Visité mis abuelos.* → ✓ *Visité a mis abuelos.* — a person after *visitar* takes *a*.\n- ✗ *¿Qué hiciste en el sábado?* → ✓ *¿Qué hiciste el sábado?* — no preposition with days.\n\n## Practice\n\nWhat do you say?\n\n1. Ask a friend how their weekend was.\n2. Say that you stayed at home and rested.\n3. Your friend Javier went to Mexico. Ask him: how long were you there?\n4. It rained all week on your friend's vacation. React.\n\nComplete.\n\n5. —¿Adónde ___ de vacaciones? —A Costa Rica.\n6. Primero visitamos el museo y ___ comimos en un restaurante.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n7. I had a great time.\n8. We went to the mountains with some friends.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. ¿Qué tal el fin de semana? / ¿Qué tal tu fin de semana? / ¿Cómo fue tu fin de semana?\n2. Me quedé en casa y descansé.\n3. ¿Cuánto tiempo estuviste allí? / ¿Cuánto tiempo estuviste en México?\n4. ¡Qué pena!\n5. fuiste\n6. después / luego\n7. Lo pasé muy bien. / La pasé muy bien.\n8. Fuimos a la montaña con unos amigos. / … con algunos amigos.\n","a2/verbs-imperfect.md":"---\nid: a2-verbs-imperfect\nlevel: A2\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: Pretérito imperfecto: descriptions and habits in the past\nsummary: Cuando era niño, jugaba en la calle. The past tense for how things used to be, what people were like and what they did regularly.\n---\n\n# Pretérito imperfecto: descriptions and habits in the past\n\nThe imperfect (*pretérito imperfecto*) describes the past instead of telling events: how things were and what people used to do. English uses \"used to\", \"would\" or the simple past: *Cuando era niño, jugaba en la calle.* (When I was a child, I used to play in the street.) Spanish usually needs no extra verb: the ending does the job. And it is the most regular tense in Spanish.\n\n## How it works\n\n| Person | hablar | comer | vivir |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | habl**aba** | com**ía** | viv**ía** |\n| tú | habl**abas** | com**ías** | viv**ías** |\n| él, ella, usted | habl**aba** | com**ía** | viv**ía** |\n| nosotros, nosotras | habl**ábamos** | com**íamos** | viv**íamos** |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | habl**abais** | com**íais** | viv**íais** |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | habl**aban** | com**ían** | viv**ían** |\n\n- *Yo* and *él / ella / usted* share one form: *hablaba*. Add the subject when it is not clear.\n- *-ía* always has a written accent. In the *-ar* forms only *nosotros* has one: *hablábamos*.\n- Stem-changing verbs are regular here: *quería, podía, tenía*.\n\n**Only three irregular verbs:**\n\n| Person | ser | ir | ver |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | **era** | **iba** | **veía** |\n| tú | **eras** | **ibas** | **veías** |\n| él, ella, usted | **era** | **iba** | **veía** |\n| nosotros, nosotras | **éramos** | **íbamos** | **veíamos** |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | **erais** | **ibais** | **veíais** |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | **eran** | **iban** | **veían** |\n\n*Hay* becomes *había* (there was / there were): *Antes había un cine aquí.*\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Habits in the past.** *Todos los veranos íbamos a la playa.* (Every summer we went to the beach.)\n2. **Descriptions of people, places and things.** *Mi abuela era muy alta y tenía el pelo blanco.* (My grandmother was very tall and had white hair.)\n3. **Age and time in the past.** *Tenía diez años. Eran las nueve de la noche.* (I was ten. It was nine p.m.)\n4. **Then and now.** *Antes vivía en el centro; ahora vivo en las afueras.* (I used to live downtown; now I live in the suburbs.)\n5. **Feelings and states.** *Estaba cansada y no quería salir.* (I was tired and didn't want to go out.)\n\n**Signal words:** *antes* (before, in the past), *de niño / de niña* (as a child), *cuando era pequeño* (when I was little), *siempre*, *todos los días*, *normalmente*.\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| De niña, vivía en un pueblo pequeño. | As a child, I lived in a small village. |\n| Mi padre trabajaba en un banco. | My father worked in a bank. |\n| Los domingos comíamos en casa de mis abuelos. | On Sundays we used to eat at my grandparents' house. |\n| La casa tenía un jardín muy bonito. | The house had a very nice garden. |\n| ¿Qué querías ser de pequeño? | What did you want to be when you were little? |\n| Antes había muchas tiendas en esta calle. | There used to be a lot of stores on this street. |\n| Era un día muy bonito. | It was a very nice day. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *De pequeño* (Spain) and *de chico* (much of Latin America) both mean \"as a child\", like *de niño*.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Usaba jugar en la calle.* → ✓ *Jugaba en la calle.* — \"used to\" is usually just the imperfect ending.\n- ✗ *Cuando fui niño, vivía en Cádiz.* → ✓ *Cuando era niño, vivía en Cádiz.* — childhood is a background description: *era*.\n- ✗ *Antes comia mucha carne.* → ✓ *Antes comía mucha carne.* — *-ía* always has a written accent.\n- ✗ *Estaba diez años.* → ✓ *Tenía diez años.* — age uses *tener*, in the past too.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the imperfect.\n\n1. De niño, yo ___ (jugar) en el parque.\n2. Mis abuelos ___ (vivir) en el campo.\n3. Nosotros ___ (ir) a la playa todos los veranos.\n4. Mi profesora ___ (ser) muy simpática.\n5. Antes ___ (haber) un mercado en la plaza.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n6. When I was little, I didn't like vegetables.\n7. My brother used to work in a hotel.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. jugaba\n2. vivían\n3. íbamos\n4. era\n5. había\n6. Cuando era pequeño (pequeña, niño, niña), no me gustaban las verduras.\n7. Mi hermano trabajaba en un hotel. / Antes mi hermano trabajaba en un hotel.\n","a2/verbs-present-perfect.md":"---\nid: a2-verbs-present-perfect\nlevel: A2\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: Pretérito perfecto; ya, todavía no, alguna vez\nsummary: He comido, ¿has estado alguna vez…? The present perfect for experiences, for things not done yet and, in Spain, for today.\n---\n\n# Pretérito perfecto; ya, todavía no, alguna vez\n\nThe present perfect (*pretérito perfecto*) is built like English \"have done\": *he comido* (I have eaten). It looks at the past from now: experiences (*¿Has estado alguna vez en México?*), things not done yet (*Todavía no he comido*) and, in Spain, things that happened today. Unlike English, nothing goes between the two parts: *Ya he comido* (I have already eaten).\n\n## How it works\n\n**haber (present) + participle**\n\n| Person | haber | + participle |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | **he** | hablado / comido / vivido |\n| tú | **has** | hablado / comido / vivido |\n| él, ella, usted | **ha** | hablado / comido / vivido |\n| nosotros, nosotras | **hemos** | hablado / comido / vivido |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | **habéis** | hablado / comido / vivido |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | **han** | hablado / comido / vivido |\n\n- Participle: *-ar* → **-ado** (*hablado*), *-er* / *-ir* → **-ido** (*comido, vivido*).\n- The participle never changes: *Ella ha comido. Ellos han comido.*\n- *Ya*, *no* and reflexive pronouns go **before** *haber*: *Ya he terminado. No he terminado. Me he levantado tarde.*\n\n**Irregular participles:**\n\n| Infinitive | Participle | Infinitive | Participle |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| hacer | **hecho** | ver | **visto** |\n| decir | **dicho** | escribir | **escrito** |\n| poner | **puesto** | abrir | **abierto** |\n| volver | **vuelto** | romper | **roto** |\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Experiences in your life (no time given).** *He estado dos veces en Perú.* (I've been to Peru twice.) *¿Has comido alguna vez pulpo?* (Have you ever eaten octopus?)\n2. **Not yet / already.** *Todavía no he visto la película.* (I haven't seen the movie yet.) *¿Ya has hecho la cena?* (Have you made dinner yet?)\n3. **Never.** *Nunca he viajado en avión.* = *No he viajado nunca en avión.* (I've never traveled by plane.)\n4. **A time that is not over (Spain).** *Hoy he trabajado mucho. Esta semana hemos ido dos veces al cine.* (Today I've worked a lot. This week we've been to the movies twice.)\n\n**Signal words:** *ya* (already, yet), *todavía no* / *aún no* (not yet), *alguna vez* (ever), *nunca* (never), *hoy*, *esta mañana*, *esta semana*, *este año*.\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Has estado alguna vez en México? | Have you ever been to Mexico? |\n| No, nunca he estado allí. | No, I've never been there. |\n| Todavía no he comido. | I haven't eaten yet. |\n| ¿Ya habéis terminado? (Spain) | Have you finished already? |\n| Mi hermana ha vivido en tres países. | My sister has lived in three countries. |\n| ¿Quién ha abierto la ventana? | Who opened the window? |\n| Esta mañana me he levantado a las seis. | This morning I got up at six. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- **Spain vs Latin America.** In Spain the present perfect is normal for today: *Hoy he desayunado tarde.* In most of Latin America people use the preterite for today and in many countries also for *ya* and *todavía no*: *Hoy desayuné tarde. ¿Ya comiste? Todavía no comí.* For experiences (*¿Has estado alguna vez…?*) the perfect is common everywhere.\n- With a finished time (*ayer, el año pasado, en 2019*), use the preterite everywhere: *Ayer comí paella*, never *ayer he comido*.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Ayer he visto a Marta.* → ✓ *Ayer vi a Marta.* — *ayer* is a finished time: preterite.\n- ✗ *¿Has hacido la cena?* → ✓ *¿Has hecho la cena?* — *hacer* has an irregular participle: *hecho*.\n- ✗ *He ya comido.* → ✓ *Ya he comido.* — nothing goes between *haber* and the participle.\n- ✗ *Ella ha comida.* → ✓ *Ella ha comido.* — the participle after *haber* does not change.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the present perfect.\n\n1. Yo nunca ___ (estar) en Argentina.\n2. ¿Tú ya ___ (hacer) la cena?\n3. Nosotros todavía no ___ (ver) la nueva serie.\n4. Mis padres ___ (volver) del viaje.\n5. ¿Quién ___ (romper) el vaso?\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n6. Have you ever eaten paella? (to a friend)\n7. I haven't finished yet.\n8. She has written three books.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. he estado\n2. has hecho\n3. hemos visto\n4. han vuelto\n5. ha roto\n6. ¿Has comido alguna vez paella? / ¿Has comido paella alguna vez? / ¿Alguna vez has comido paella?\n7. Todavía no he terminado. / No he terminado todavía. / Todavía no terminé.\n8. Ha escrito tres libros. / Ella ha escrito tres libros.\n","a2/verbs-preterite-irregular.md":"---\nid: a2-verbs-preterite-irregular\nlevel: A2\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: Pretérito indefinido: common irregular verbs\nsummary: Fui, hice, tuve, estuve, dije, pude, vine: the irregular preterites you need every day, with new stems and no written accents.\n---\n\n# Pretérito indefinido: common irregular verbs\n\nThe most frequent verbs are irregular in the preterite, just as English has \"went\", \"did\" and \"had\". Most of them follow one pattern: a new stem (*tener → tuv-*) and one set of endings **without written accents**: *tuve, tuvo*. *Ser* and *ir* share their forms, so *fui* means both \"I was\" and \"I went\".\n\n## How it works\n\n**ser and ir: the same forms**\n\n| Person | ser / ir |\n| --- | --- |\n| yo | **fui** |\n| tú | **fuiste** |\n| él, ella, usted | **fue** |\n| nosotros, nosotras | **fuimos** |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | **fuisteis** |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | **fueron** |\n\n*Fui a Lima.* (I went to Lima.) *Fue un viaje largo.* (It was a long trip.)\n\n**A new stem + -e, -iste, -o, -imos, -isteis, -ieron**\n\n| Infinitive | Stem | yo | él, ella, usted | ellos, ellas, ustedes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| tener | **tuv-** | tuve | tuvo | tuvieron |\n| estar | **estuv-** | estuve | estuvo | estuvieron |\n| poder | **pud-** | pude | pudo | pudieron |\n| poner | **pus-** | puse | puso | pusieron |\n| venir | **vin-** | vine | vino | vinieron |\n| hacer | **hic-** | hice | **hizo** | hicieron |\n\n- No written accents: *tuve, pudo, hizo*.\n- *Hacer* writes **z** in *hizo* (to keep the sound).\n\n**Stems in -j: the ellos form ends in -eron**\n\n| Infinitive | Stem | yo | él, ella, usted | ellos, ellas, ustedes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| decir | **dij-** | dije | dijo | dij**eron** |\n| traer | **traj-** | traje | trajo | traj**eron** |\n\n**dar and ver** are short and have no accents: *di, diste, dio, dimos, disteis, dieron*; *vi, viste, vio, vimos, visteis, vieron*.\n\n**-ir verbs with a stem change** change only in the third person: *pedir → pidió, pidieron*; *dormir → durmió, durmieron*; also *preferir → prefirió*. The other forms are regular: *pedí, dormí*.\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Where you went.** *El verano pasado fuimos a Cartagena.* (Last summer we went to Cartagena.)\n2. **How something was, as a whole.** *La fiesta fue muy divertida.* (The party was a lot of fun.)\n3. **Where you were, and for how long.** *Estuve dos semanas en Perú.* (I was in Peru for two weeks.)\n4. **What you did, had or could do.** *Hice la cena. No pude venir.* (I made dinner. I couldn't come.)\n5. **What someone said.** *Ana dijo que sí.* (Ana said yes.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Qué hiciste el sábado? | What did you do on Saturday? |\n| Fui al cine con Diego. | I went to the movies with Diego. |\n| Mis primos vinieron a cenar. | My cousins came for dinner. |\n| Estuvimos en casa todo el día. | We were at home all day. |\n| ¿Por qué no pudiste venir? | Why couldn't you come? |\n| Ayer tuve un examen. | I had an exam yesterday. |\n| ¿Qué dijo el profesor? | What did the teacher say? |\n| Sofía durmió nueve horas. | Sofía slept for nine hours. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- For an event, *fue* and *estuvo* are both common: *La fiesta fue / estuvo genial.* (The party was great.) For where someone was, only *estar*: *Estuve en Madrid.*\n- *Hay* becomes *hubo*: *Hubo un accidente en la autopista.* (There was an accident on the highway.)\n- *Ser* or *ir*? *Fue a* + a place is \"went\"; *fue* + a description is \"was\".\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Ayer tení un examen.* → ✓ *Ayer tuve un examen.* — *tener* has its own stem, *tuv-*.\n- ✗ *Mi padre hizó la cena.* → ✓ *Mi padre hizo la cena.* — irregular preterites have no written accent.\n- ✗ *Mis amigos dijieron que no.* → ✓ *Mis amigos dijeron que no.* — after *j*, the ending is *-eron*.\n- ✗ *Yo fue a la playa.* → ✓ *Yo fui a la playa.* — *fue* is for *él, ella, usted*.\n- ✗ *Lucía pedió una pizza.* → ✓ *Lucía pidió una pizza.* — *pedir* changes *e → i* in *él, ella, usted*.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the preterite.\n\n1. El verano pasado nosotros ___ (ir) a Perú.\n2. ¿Qué ___ (hacer) tú ayer?\n3. Mi madre ___ (estar) dos días en el hospital.\n4. Ellos no ___ (poder) venir.\n5. Yo ___ (tener) un problema en el trabajo.\n6. ¿Qué ___ (decir) los profesores?\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n7. The concert was very long.\n8. My friends came on Sunday.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. fuimos\n2. hiciste\n3. estuvo\n4. pudieron\n5. tuve\n6. dijeron\n7. El concierto fue muy largo.\n8. Mis amigos vinieron el domingo. / El domingo vinieron mis amigos.\n","a2/verbs-preterite-regular.md":"---\nid: a2-verbs-preterite-regular\nlevel: A2\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: Pretérito indefinido: regular verbs\nsummary: Hablé, comiste, vivió: the simple past for finished actions, with ayer, anoche and el año pasado.\n---\n\n# Pretérito indefinido: regular verbs\n\nThe preterite (*pretérito indefinido*) is the Spanish simple past: *Ayer hablé con Marta.* (Yesterday I talked to Marta.) It tells what happened at a finished moment, like English \"talked\" or \"ate\". English needs \"did\" for questions and negatives; Spanish does not: *¿Hablaste con ella?* (Did you talk to her?) Watch the accents: *hablo* is \"I speak\", *habló* is \"he / she / you spoke\".\n\n## How it works\n\nDrop *-ar*, *-er* or *-ir* and add the ending; *-er* and *-ir* share one set.\n\n| Person | hablar | comer | vivir |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| yo | habl**é** | com**í** | viv**í** |\n| tú | habl**aste** | com**iste** | viv**iste** |\n| él, ella, usted | habl**ó** | com**ió** | viv**ió** |\n| nosotros, nosotras | habl**amos** | com**imos** | viv**imos** |\n| vosotros, vosotras (Spain) | habl**asteis** | com**isteis** | viv**isteis** |\n| ellos, ellas, ustedes | habl**aron** | com**ieron** | viv**ieron** |\n\n- The *yo* and *él / ella / usted* forms have a written accent: *hablé, habló, comí, comió*.\n- *Hablamos* and *vivimos* look like the present; the time word tells you which: *Ayer hablamos.*\n- The *tú* form has no *-s* at the end: *hablaste*.\n- Stem-changing verbs in *-ar* and *-er* do not change in the preterite: *cerrar → cerré*, *volver → volví*, *jugar → jugué*.\n\n**Spelling changes in the yo form:**\n\n| Infinitive | yo | él, ella, usted |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| buscar, tocar (-car) | bus**qué**, to**qué** | buscó, tocó |\n| llegar, pagar (-gar) | lle**gué**, pa**gué** | llegó, pagó |\n| empezar (-zar) | empe**cé** | empezó |\n\n*Leer*: *le**yó***, *le**yeron***.\n\n**Time words:** *ayer* (yesterday), *anteayer* (the day before yesterday), *anoche* (last night), *el lunes pasado* (last Monday), *la semana pasada* (last week), *el año pasado* (last year), *hace dos años* (two years ago).\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **A finished action.** *Ayer compré pan.* (Yesterday I bought bread.)\n2. **Actions one after the other.** *Llegué a casa, cené y me acosté.* (I got home, had dinner and went to bed.)\n3. **A closed period.** *Viví tres años en Lima.* (I lived in Lima for three years.)\n4. **Questions and negatives, without \"did\".** *¿Cenaste en casa? —No, no cené.* (Did you have dinner at home? No.)\n5. **\"Ago\": *hace*.** *Terminé la universidad hace cinco años.* (I finished college five years ago.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Anoche cenamos en un restaurante peruano. | Last night we had dinner at a Peruvian restaurant. |\n| ¿A qué hora llegaste? | What time did you arrive? |\n| Mis padres vivieron dos años en Chile. | My parents lived in Chile for two years. |\n| Ayer no bebí café. | I didn't drink coffee yesterday. |\n| El concierto empezó a las nueve. | The concert started at nine. |\n| Busqué las llaves por toda la casa. | I looked for the keys all over the house. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- In Spain, for things that happened earlier today (*hoy*, *esta mañana*), people usually use another past tense: see [[a2-verbs-present-perfect]]. In most of Latin America the preterite is normal for today too: *Hoy hablé con Marta.* With *ayer* or *el año pasado*, everyone uses the preterite.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Ella hablo con el profesor.* → ✓ *Ella habló con el profesor.* — without the accent, *hablo* means \"I speak\".\n- ✗ *¿Comistes en casa?* → ✓ *¿Comiste en casa?* — the *tú* form of the preterite has no final *-s*.\n- ✗ *Ayer como con mis amigos.* → ✓ *Ayer comí con mis amigos.* — a finished past action needs the preterite.\n- ✗ *Anoche comé pizza.* → ✓ *Anoche comí pizza.* — *-er* verbs take *-í*, not the *-ar* ending *-é*.\n- ✗ *Buscé las llaves.* → ✓ *Busqué las llaves.* — *-car* becomes *-qué* in the *yo* form.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the preterite.\n\n1. Ayer yo ___ (trabajar) hasta las seis.\n2. ¿Tú ___ (comer) en casa anoche?\n3. Mi hermano ___ (vivir) dos años en Bogotá.\n4. Yo ___ (pagar) la cuenta.\n5. La película ___ (empezar) tarde.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n6. Did you buy bread? (to a friend)\n7. I finished my work two hours ago.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. trabajé\n2. comiste\n3. vivió\n4. pagué\n5. empezó\n6. ¿Compraste pan?\n7. Terminé mi trabajo hace dos horas. / Hace dos horas terminé mi trabajo.\n","a2/verbs-preterite-vs-imperfect-basics.md":"---\nid: a2-verbs-preterite-vs-imperfect-basics\nlevel: A2\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: Indefinido vs imperfecto: first contrast\nsummary: Llovía cuando salí de casa. The imperfect sets the scene, the preterite tells what happened.\n---\n\n# Indefinido vs imperfecto: first contrast\n\nSpanish has two simple past tenses, and English has one. \"It rained\" can be *llovió* or *llovía*, and the choice depends on what you want to say. Think of a film: the imperfect (*llovía*) describes the scene in the background; the preterite (*salí*) is the action that moves the story on. *Llovía cuando salí de casa.* (It was raining when I left home.)\n\n## How it works\n\n| Imperfect (*hablaba*) | Preterite (*hablé*) |\n| --- | --- |\n| the scene: what things were like | the events: what happened |\n| an action in progress, without an end | a finished action, often with a start or an end |\n| a habit: what used to happen | one time, or a counted number of times |\n| *siempre, todos los días, normalmente, antes* | *ayer, una vez, el lunes, de repente* (suddenly), *tres veces* |\n\n**The basic pattern:** an action in progress (imperfect) is interrupted by a new event (preterite).\n\n*Cenábamos **cuando** llamó Ana.* (We were having dinner when Ana called.)\n\n- The imperfect can often be translated \"was / were + -ing\": *Leía* = I was reading.\n- The same verb changes meaning with the tense: *Fue un día largo* (the day as a whole, now over) / *Era un día de sol* (description of the setting).\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Background + event.** *Hacía sol cuando llegamos a la playa.* (It was sunny when we got to the beach.)\n2. **Interrupted action.** *Dormía cuando sonó el teléfono.* (I was sleeping when the phone rang.)\n3. **Habit vs one time.** *Siempre comíamos paella los domingos, pero ese domingo comimos pizza.* (We always had paella on Sundays, but that Sunday we had pizza.)\n4. **Description vs action in a story.** *La casa era vieja y oscura. Abrí la puerta y entré.* (The house was old and dark. I opened the door and went in.)\n5. **A closed period counts as an event.** *Viví en Roma tres años.* (I lived in Rome for three years.) But: *Cuando vivía en Roma, iba mucho al cine.* (When I lived in Rome, I often went to the movies.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Llovía cuando salí de casa. | It was raining when I left home. |\n| Mientras yo cocinaba, Pablo puso la mesa. | While I was cooking, Pablo set the table. |\n| Eran las once cuando terminó la película. | It was eleven when the movie ended. |\n| Antes iba al gimnasio, pero el año pasado empecé a correr. | I used to go to the gym, but last year I started running. |\n| Estaba muy cansada, así que me acosté temprano. | I was very tired, so I went to bed early. |\n| Conocí a Laura cuando trabajaba en Bogotá. | I met Laura when I was working in Bogotá. |\n| De repente, empezó a llover. | Suddenly, it started to rain. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *Mientras* (while) is usually followed by the imperfect: *Mientras esperaba el autobús, leí las noticias.*\n- *Cuando* can go with both. Ask yourself: is it a background (imperfect) or an event (preterite)? *Cuando era joven…* / *Cuando llegué…*\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Cuando llegué a casa, mi madre cocinó.* → ✓ *Cuando llegué a casa, mi madre cocinaba.* — she was in the middle of it: imperfect.\n- ✗ *De niño fui a la playa todos los veranos.* → ✓ *De niño iba a la playa todos los veranos.* — a repeated habit takes the imperfect.\n- ✗ *Ayer veía una película muy buena.* → ✓ *Ayer vi una película muy buena.* — one finished action yesterday: preterite.\n- ✗ *Estaba en Roma tres días.* → ✓ *Estuve en Roma tres días.* — a closed period is an event: preterite.\n\n## Practice\n\nChoose the imperfect or the preterite.\n\n1. Ayer ___ (llover) todo el día.\n2. Cuando era pequeña, ___ (tener) un perro.\n3. ___ (ducharse, yo) cuando llegó el cartero.\n4. El sábado pasado ___ (ir, nosotros) al cine.\n5. Antes Carlos ___ (trabajar) los fines de semana.\n6. La habitación ___ (ser) pequeña, pero ___ (tener) mucha luz.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n7. I was reading when you called. (to a friend)\n\n## Answers\n\n1. llovió\n2. tenía\n3. Me duchaba\n4. fuimos\n5. trabajaba\n6. era, tenía\n7. Leía cuando llamaste. / Leía cuando me llamaste.\n","a2/vocabulary-free-time-weather.md":"---\nid: a2-vocabulary-free-time-weather\nlevel: A2\ncategory: vocabulary\ntitle: Free time, sports and weather\nsummary: Hobbies, sports and going out, and how to talk about the weather with hace, llueve and está nublado.\n---\n\n# Free time, sports and weather\n\nThe weather works differently in Spanish. English says \"it is hot\"; Spanish says \"it makes heat\": *hace calor*. Rain and snow have their own verbs: *llueve* (it's raining), *nieva* (it's snowing). For hobbies, watch the verbs: you *juegas* a game or a sport, but you *tocas* an instrument. In the past: *Hacía sol, así que fuimos a la playa.*\n\n## Words\n\n**Weather**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| ¿Qué tiempo hace? | What's the weather like? |\n| hace calor / hace frío | it's hot / it's cold |\n| hace sol / hace viento | it's sunny / it's windy |\n| hace buen tiempo / hace mal tiempo | the weather is good / bad |\n| llueve (llover) | it rains, it's raining |\n| nieva (nevar) | it snows, it's snowing |\n| está nublado | it's cloudy |\n| hay niebla | it's foggy |\n| la lluvia / la nieve | the rain / the snow |\n| la tormenta | the storm |\n| la temperatura | the temperature |\n| Estamos a 30 grados. / Hace 30 grados. | It's 30 degrees. |\n\n**Sports**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el deporte | the sport |\n| jugar al fútbol / al tenis (Latin America also: jugar fútbol) | to play soccer / tennis |\n| el baloncesto (Spain) / el básquetbol (Latin America) | basketball |\n| nadar / la natación | to swim / swimming |\n| correr | to run, to go running |\n| montar en bicicleta (Spain) / andar en bicicleta (Latin America) | to ride a bike |\n| ir al gimnasio | to go to the gym |\n| hacer senderismo | to go hiking |\n| el partido | the game, the match |\n| el equipo | the team |\n\n**Free time**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el tiempo libre | free time |\n| salir con amigos | to go out with friends |\n| quedar con alguien (Spain) | to meet up with someone |\n| ir al cine / al teatro / a un concierto | to go to the movies / the theater / a concert |\n| ver una serie / una película | to watch a series / a movie |\n| leer / escuchar música | to read / to listen to music |\n| tocar la guitarra / el piano | to play the guitar / the piano |\n| bailar / cantar | to dance / to sing |\n| pasear | to go for a walk |\n| ir de compras | to go shopping |\n| cocinar | to cook |\n| la afición (Spain) / el pasatiempo | the hobby |\n\n## Word partners\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| En verano hace mucho calor. | It's very hot in summer. |\n| Ayer llovió todo el día. | It rained all day yesterday. |\n| Cuando salimos, hacía mucho viento. | When we went out, it was very windy. |\n| Los sábados juego al tenis con mi hermano. | On Saturdays I play tennis with my brother. |\n| De niña tocaba el piano. | As a child I played the piano. |\n| ¿Has visto el partido? | Have you seen the game? |\n| Me gusta pasear por el parque. | I like going for a walk in the park. |\n| Mañana va a nevar en la montaña. | It's going to snow in the mountains tomorrow. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *Hace* + noun: *mucho* goes with it, not *muy*: *hace **mucho** calor*, *hace **mucho** frío*. But *muy* with an adjective: *Está muy nublado.*\n- *El tiempo* means both \"the weather\" and \"time\": *¿Qué tiempo hace?* / *No tengo tiempo.*\n- People feel cold or hot with *tener*: *Tengo frío.* (I'm cold.)\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Es calor hoy.* → ✓ *Hace calor hoy.* — weather takes *hacer*.\n- ✗ *Estoy caliente.* → ✓ *Tengo calor.* — \"I'm hot\" is *tengo calor*; *estoy caliente* has a sexual meaning.\n- ✗ *Juego la guitarra.* → ✓ *Toco la guitarra.* — instruments take *tocar*.\n- ✗ *Hace muy calor.* → ✓ *Hace mucho calor.* — *calor* is a noun, so *mucho*.\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete.\n\n1. En invierno ___ mucho frío.\n2. Mi hermano ___ el piano muy bien.\n3. Hoy no hace sol: está ___.\n4. Los domingos ___ al fútbol con mis amigos.\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. It's very hot today.\n6. I'm cold.\n7. It snowed a lot last winter.\n8. When I was a child, I swam every day.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. hace\n2. toca\n3. nublado\n4. juego\n5. Hoy hace mucho calor. / Hace mucho calor hoy.\n6. Tengo frío.\n7. El invierno pasado nevó mucho. / Nevó mucho el invierno pasado.\n8. Cuando era niño, nadaba todos los días. / Cuando era niña, nadaba todos los días.\n","a2/vocabulary-travel-transport.md":"---\nid: a2-vocabulary-travel-transport\nlevel: A2\ncategory: vocabulary\ntitle: Travel, transportation and accommodation\nsummary: Trains, buses, planes, tickets and hotel rooms, with the Spain and Latin American words side by side.\n---\n\n# Travel, transportation and accommodation\n\nWith the preterite you can now tell the story of a trip: *Tomé el tren de las ocho, pero llegué tarde.* Travel words differ between Spain and Latin America, so both are given. One verb needs care: *coger* (to take a bus) is normal in Spain but rude in parts of Latin America; *tomar* works everywhere.\n\n## Words\n\n**Getting around**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el viaje | the trip |\n| el tren | the train |\n| el autobús (Mexico: el camión; Argentina: el colectivo) | the bus |\n| el metro | the subway |\n| el avión | the plane |\n| el barco | the boat, the ship |\n| el coche (Spain) / el carro (Latin America) / el auto (Argentina, Chile) | the car |\n| el taxi | the taxi |\n| la bicicleta | the bicycle |\n| a pie | on foot |\n\n**At the station and the airport**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| la estación | the station |\n| el aeropuerto | the airport |\n| el andén | the platform |\n| el billete (Spain) / el boleto (Latin America) | the ticket |\n| de ida / de ida y vuelta | one-way / round-trip |\n| el horario | the timetable, the schedule |\n| la salida / la llegada | the departure / the arrival |\n| el vuelo | the flight |\n| la puerta de embarque | the gate |\n| la tarjeta de embarque (Spain) / el pase de abordar (Mexico, Central America) | the boarding pass |\n| el pasaporte | the passport |\n| la maleta (Argentina: la valija) | the suitcase |\n| el equipaje | the luggage |\n| el retraso (Latin America also: la demora) | the delay |\n\n**Where you stay**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| el hotel | the hotel |\n| el hostal | the guesthouse, the hostel |\n| la habitación individual (Latin America also: sencilla) / doble | the single / double room |\n| la reserva (Latin America also: la reservación) | the reservation |\n| la recepción | the front desk |\n| la llave | the key |\n| desayuno incluido | breakfast included |\n\n**Verbs**\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| viajar | to travel |\n| tomar (Spain also: coger) | to take (a bus, a train) |\n| salir (de) | to leave (from) |\n| llegar (a) | to arrive (in, at) |\n| perder (el tren) | to miss (the train) |\n| reservar | to book |\n| quedarse (en) | to stay (at) |\n| hacer la maleta (Latin America also: empacar) | to pack |\n| facturar (Spain) / documentar (Mexico) | to check (a bag) |\n\n## Word partners\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Tomé el tren de las ocho. | I took the eight o'clock train. |\n| Perdimos el autobús y llegamos tarde. | We missed the bus and arrived late. |\n| El vuelo salió con una hora de retraso. | The flight left an hour late. |\n| Llegué a Madrid el lunes. | I arrived in Madrid on Monday. |\n| Reservé una habitación doble. | I booked a double room. |\n| Compré un billete de ida y vuelta. (Spain) | I bought a round-trip ticket. |\n| Fuimos en avión. | We went by plane. |\n| Volví a casa a pie. | I walked back home. |\n| Nos quedamos en un hotel cerca de la playa. | We stayed at a hotel near the beach. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- Transportation takes *en* (*en tren, en avión, en coche*), but \"on foot\" is *a pie*.\n- The place you arrive in takes *a*: *llegar a Lima*, *llegar al hotel*. English says \"in\" or \"at\"; Spanish has only *a*.\n- *El billete* also means a banknote; in Latin America a ticket is *el boleto* or *el pasaje*.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *Llegué en Madrid a las diez.* → ✓ *Llegué a Madrid a las diez.* — the place you arrive in takes *a*.\n- ✗ *Fuimos por avión.* → ✓ *Fuimos en avión.* — transportation takes *en*.\n- ✗ *Volví en pie.* → ✓ *Volví a pie.* — \"on foot\" is *a pie*.\n- ✗ *una doble habitación* → ✓ *una habitación doble* — *doble* and *individual* go after the noun.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the word with *el* or *la*.\n\n1. ticket (Spain)\n2. platform\n3. flight\n4. suitcase\n\nTranslate into Spanish.\n\n5. I missed the train.\n6. We arrived in Lima on Friday.\n7. A round-trip ticket, please. (Spain)\n8. She went to work by bus.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. el billete\n2. el andén\n3. el vuelo\n4. la maleta / la valija\n5. Perdí el tren.\n6. Llegamos a Lima el viernes. / El viernes llegamos a Lima.\n7. Un billete de ida y vuelta, por favor.\n8. Fue al trabajo en autobús. / Fue a trabajar en autobús. (*en camión*, *en colectivo*)\n"},"summaries":{"A1":"---\nid: a1-summary\nlevel: A1\ntitle: A1 in a nutshell\nnext: A2\n---\n\n## A1 in a nutshell\n\n**What you can do at this level**\n\nYou can introduce yourself and others, say where you are from and what you do, and ask the same questions back. You can talk about your family, your home and your town, your day and your plans, and you can order in a café, buy food and ask what things cost. You understand people who speak slowly and clearly, and you know how to ask them to repeat.\n\n**Key ideas**\n\n- **Two verbs for \"to be\".** *Ser* says what something is (*Soy profesora*, *Es de Chile*); *estar* says where it is and how it is now (*Estoy en casa*, *Estoy cansada*).\n- **Endings show the person.** *Hablo, hablas, habla…*: the pronoun is usually left out.\n- **Every noun is masculine or feminine,** and articles and adjectives agree with it: *la casa blanca*, *los coches rojos*.\n- **Gustar works backwards:** the thing you like is the subject. *Me gusta el café. Me gustan los gatos.*\n- **Ir a + infinitive** is your first future: *Voy a estudiar.*\n\n**The most common trap**\n\nTranslating \"to be\" word for word. Age, hunger and cold take *tener* (*Tengo 30 años*, *tengo hambre*), location takes *estar*, and a job takes *ser* without *un / una* (*Es médico*).\n\n**How long it takes**\n\n- Reaching A1 from zero usually takes **about 80–100 hours** of guided study plus practice on your own.\n- With **3–4 hours a week**, that is roughly **5–7 months**. Speaking a little every day helps more than one long session a week.\n\n*These are typical ranges for adult learners; your pace depends on how much time you spend with the language.*\n\n**What next**\n\nYou are ready for A2 when you can talk for two or three minutes about yourself, your family and your day, and ask someone a few questions about theirs. At A2 you will learn to talk about the past (*ayer comí*, *de niño vivía*) and to describe things in more detail.\n"},"cheatsheets":{"A1":"---\nid: cheatsheet-a1\nlevel: A1\ntitle: A1 cheat sheet\nsummary: The forms, rules, phrases and traps of A1 on one page.\n---\n\n# A1 cheat sheet\n\n## Forms\n\n| Form | Pattern | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ser | soy, eres, es, somos, sois, son | *Soy de Lima. Es médica.* |\n| estar | estoy, estás, está, estamos, estáis, están | *Estoy en casa. Está cansado.* |\n| tener | tengo, tienes, tiene, tenemos, tenéis, tienen | *Tengo dos hermanos.* |\n| -ar / -er / -ir | -o, -as/-es, -a/-e, -amos/-emos/-imos, -áis/-éis/-ís, -an/-en | *hablo, comes, vivimos* |\n| stem changes | e→ie, o→ue, e→i (not nosotros, vosotros) | *quiero, puedo, pido* |\n| yo in -go | hago, salgo, pongo, tengo, traigo | *Salgo a las ocho.* |\n| ir (a) | voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van + a + infinitive | *Voy a estudiar.* |\n| reflexive | me, te, se, nos, os, se + verb | *Me levanto a las siete.* |\n\n## Grammar\n\n- **Articles:** *el, la, los, las* / *un, una, unos, unas*; *a + el = al*, *de + el = del*. No article with a job: *Es profesor.*\n- **Agreement:** *el chico alto*, *la chica alta*, *los chicos altos*; colors after the noun: *un coche rojo*.\n- **ser / estar:** what it is (ser) vs where it is and how it is now (estar). Events: *La fiesta es en mi casa.*\n- **hay / está:** *Hay un banco* (something new) / *El banco está allí* (a known thing).\n- **gustar:** *me / te / le / nos / os / les* + *gusta* (one thing, an action) / *gustan* (plural).\n- **Questions and no:** *¿Dónde vives? No vivo aquí. No tengo nada.* Question words have accents.\n- **Possessives / demonstratives:** *mi, tu, su, nuestro*; *este, ese, aquel*.\n\n## Phrases\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Hola, me llamo… Mucho gusto. | Hi, my name is… Nice to meet you. |\n| ¿De dónde eres? —Soy de… | Where are you from? —I'm from… |\n| ¿Cuántos años tienes? —Tengo… años. | How old are you? —I'm… |\n| ¿A qué te dedicas? —Soy… | What do you do? —I'm a… |\n| ¿Qué hora es? —Son las tres y media. | What time is it? —It's half past three. |\n| ¿A qué hora abre? | What time does it open? |\n| ¿Hay un banco por aquí? | Is there a bank around here? |\n| Quería un café, por favor. | I'd like a coffee, please. |\n| ¿Cuánto cuesta? / ¿Cuánto es? | How much is it? |\n| La cuenta, por favor. | The check, please. |\n| ¿Puede repetir, por favor? | Can you repeat that, please? |\n| Más despacio, por favor. | Slower, please. |\n| ¿Cómo se dice … en español? | How do you say … in Spanish? |\n| Me gusta… / No me gusta… | I like… / I don't like… |\n\n## Signal words\n\n| Spanish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| siempre / a veces / nunca | always / sometimes / never |\n| todos los días / los lunes | every day / on Mondays |\n| por la mañana / tarde / noche | in the morning / afternoon / at night |\n| a las ocho | at eight |\n| hoy / mañana / esta noche | today / tomorrow / tonight |\n| el fin de semana | on the weekend |\n| en julio / en verano | in July / in summer |\n| aquí / ahí / allí (Latin America also acá / allá) | here / there / over there |\n\n## Watch out\n\n- ✗ *Soy 25 años.* → ✓ *Tengo 25 años.*\n- ✗ *Soy cansado.* → ✓ *Estoy cansado.*\n- ✗ *Yo gusto el café.* → ✓ *Me gusta el café.*\n- ✗ *la casa blanco* → ✓ *la casa blanca*\n- ✗ *en el lunes* → ✓ *el lunes*\n"},"home":"---\nid: home\ntitle: Spanish Levels Map\nsummary: Your map of Spanish, A1–C2, explained in English.\n---\n\n# Spanish Levels Map\n\nA map of Spanish from A1 to C2, with every explanation in English. For each level it shows what you need to know, explains each point with examples, and lets you practice it. You tick off what you know and see your way to the top.\n\n## How to start\n\n- **Not sure of your level?** [Take the placement test (6 minutes)](#/placement). It tells you where to start on the trail.\n- **Starting from zero?** [Start the trail at A1](#/trail). The first stop is the alphabet, pronunciation and stress.\n- **Looking for one thing?** Press `/` and search, or open a level in the menu.\n\n## Levels at a glance\n\n| Level | Land | Goal | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | Meadow | Introduce yourself and handle simple everyday situations when people speak slowly. | available |\n| A2 | Forest | Get by in everyday situations: travel, shopping, plans and simple stories about the past. | coming soon |\n| B1 | Hills | Talk in connected sentences about experiences, plans and opinions; cope with most travel situations. | coming soon |\n| B2 | Lake | Talk in detail with native speakers, argue a point and follow the main ideas of complex texts. | coming soon |\n| C1 | Mountains | Express yourself flexibly and precisely in social, academic and work settings. | coming soon |\n| C2 | Summit | Understand almost everything and express fine shades of meaning with ease. | coming soon |\n\nLevels marked \"coming soon\" are being written. You can already see their topics.\n\n## How it works\n\n- **The trail.** Each level is a land with stops, one per topic, and a checkpoint at the end of each unit. Read the page, take the four-question quiz and move on. You can skip what you already know.\n- **I know this.** Tick a topic when you are sure of it. Quizzes never tick it for you: the list shows what *you* think you know.\n- **Review.** Mistakes from quizzes and sets go to your mistake log and come back later, together with your flashcards. **Today** shows what is due and where you are on the trail.\n- **Tense & Mood Lab.** Every tense and mood on a timeline, tricky pairs side by side, and a few questions that tell you which form fits.\n\n## Privacy\n\nNo accounts, no tracking. Your progress is stored only in this browser. Use **Profile → Export data** to keep a copy or to move it to another device.\n\n## Found a mistake?\n\nThe content is new and checked carefully, but mistakes happen. Every topic page, quiz and set has a **Report a mistake** link at the bottom. Thank you!\n"};
